    "Applying $\\bC_t\\bv = \\lambda\\bv + \\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bigl[\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top\\bv\\bigr]$ \\emph{naively} requires one curvature--vector product (CVP) per historical sample per CG step, so a \\emph{single action's} solve costs $O(It)$ sample-CVPs. A round that solves the $K_t$ candidate systems separately under a common budget $I$ therefore costs $O(K_tIt)$ sample-CVPs, and an explicit final original-system residual check is charged separately, at $t{-}1$ further sample-CVPs per checked action. Each CVP evaluates one Jacobian--vector product (forward mode) followed by one vector--Jacobian product (reverse mode) through the network, at roughly the cost of one gradient evaluation (one JVP plus one VJP)\\footnote{Implementations based on double-backward Hessian--vector products cost closer to two gradient-equivalents per CVP; the constant does not affect the $O(\\cdot)$ accounting.}; we do not store per-sample gradient features~$\\bg_s$, only a constant number of $O(d)$ CG vectors for CG, with CVPs computed on the fly. We describe three strategies that limit the per-step cost to $|\\mathcal{S}_t|$ CVPs:",
    'At $t=1$ the lower blocks have zero rows. All square roots and inverse square roots in this statement are principal. Then:',
