    'At round~$t$ the context $\\bx_t$, finite size $K_t$, and padded measurable enumeration $a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,A_{\\max}}$ are revealed, with $\\cA_t=\\{a_{t,k}:k\\le K_t\\}$; the score-building part of $\\Omega_t$ is drawn; the agent selects $a_t$; any optional selected-action verification is completed and the oracle inequality is rechecked when required; then the reward $r_t=\\mu^*(\\bx_t,a_t)+\\eta_t$ is observed. Finally $U_{t+1}$ is drawn and $\\btheta_{t+1}$ is computed. $\\cH_t^-$ is the history immediately before the first randomized certificate draw; after all pre-reward draws, the realized operator, schedules, verification outputs, and action are $\\cHist_t$-measurable. Assume, as part of the legacy solver/oracle premise, that for every active index the realized score $S_{t,k}:=\\widehat\\mu_t(a_{t,k})+w_t(a_{t,k})$ in \\Cref{alg:ccucb} is $\\cHist_t$-measurable. Its finite maximum and smallest maximizing index $k_t$ are measurable, so $a_t=a_{t,k_t}$ is $\\cHist_t$-measurable and hence $\\cG_t=\\cHist_t$; we retain the symbol $\\cG_t$ where the played action is used. The enumeration also makes $\\cA_t$ a graph-measurable finite correspondence. The following are all $\\cHist_t$-measurable (i.e.\\ determined before the reward $r_t$): $K_t$, the candidate enumeration and derived set $\\cA_t$, and every candidate feature $\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a)$, $a\\in\\cA_t$; the chosen action $a_t$; the algorithmic curvature operator~$\\Calg_t$ (\\Cref{sec:method}); the confidence schedule $\\bar\\beta_t$; the centering certificate~$\\bar\\psi_t$ (\\Cref{asm:optim}); the per-round CG tolerance~$\\bar\\varepsilon_t$ together with any condition-number certificate used by the CG stopping rule; and the one-sided optimism-transfer factor~$u_t(a)$ (\\Cref{asm:transfer}). Since $\\btheta_t$ is $\\cF_{t-1}\\subseteq\\cHist_t$-measurable, each candidate design vector $\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a)$ is $\\cHist_t$-measurable and the realized design vector $\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)$ is $\\cG_t$-measurable. We assume $\\{U_s\\}_{s\\ge1}$ is independent of the reward noise $\\{\\eta_s\\}_{s\\ge1}$, so that revealing $U_{t+1}$ and forming $\\btheta_{t+1}$ after $r_t$ preserves the conditional sub-Gaussian property of \\Cref{asm:noise} for the future noise $\\{\\eta_s\\}_{s>t}$. Index every randomized certificate source (operator sketch, transfer bound, condition bound, or other randomized oracle) by $j$ in a finite or countable set $\\mathcal J$. For its round-$t$ validity event $\\cE_{j,t}$ choose an $\\cH_t^-$-measurable budget $\\delta_{j,t}\\ge0$ before the corresponding draw. Let $\\mathcal P_{j,t}$ be the sigma-algebra immediately before that draw: it equals $\\cH_t^-$ for the first round-start draw and includes earlier randomization, and possibly $a_t$, for later draws. Require \\[ \\Pr(\\cE_{j,t}^c\\mid\\mathcal P_{j,t})\\le\\delta_{j,t}. \\] Also require \\[ \\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\sum_{t=1}^T\\delta_{j,t} \\le\\delta_{\\rm cert},\\qquad \\cE_{\\rm cert}:=\\bigcap_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\bigcap_{t=1}^T\\cE_{j,t}. \\] The tower property and union bound yield $\\Pr(\\cE_{\\rm cert})\\ge1-\\delta_{\\rm cert}$. Deterministic or almost-sure certificates use $\\delta_{j,t}=0$. The self-normalized confidence event is separate and receives probability $\\delta$. Countability makes the joint event measurable, makes the nonnegative double sum well-defined, and permits the stated union bound. When \\Cref{lem:bounded-output-residual} supplies the residual-energy envelope, include its time-uniform event $\\cE_R$ as one certificate source and reserve $\\delta_R$ inside $\\delta_{\\rm cert}$ (equivalently, require $\\delta_R+\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\sum_{t=1}^T\\delta_{j,t} \\le\\delta_{\\rm cert}$ for the remaining sources). The residual event is never counted again in the self-normalized failure probability $\\delta$.',
    'Because $A_T$ is a sum of $T$ rank-one matrices, $\\operatorname{rank}(A_T)\\le T$, and the stated feature bound gives $\\operatorname{tr}(A_T)\\le TG^2/\\sigma^2$. If $r_T>0$, concavity of $x\\mapsto\\log(1+x/\\lambda)$ on the leading eigenvalues gives \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^{r}\\log(1+\\nu_i/\\lambda) =\\sum_{i=1}^{r_T}\\log(1+\\nu_i/\\lambda) \\le r_T\\log\\!\\left(1+ \\frac{\\tau_T-\\Delta_{T,r}}{r_T\\lambda}\\right). \\] The equality uses zero eigenvalues when $r>T$. For the tail, at most $q_{T,r}$ eigenvalues can be nonzero: there are at most $d-r$ tail coordinates and at most $T-r$ nonzero eigenvalues after the first $r$. Concavity, with zero padding when necessary, therefore gives \\[ \\sum_{i>r}\\log(1+\\nu_i/\\lambda) \\le q_{T,r}\\log\\!\\left(1+ \\frac{\\Delta_{T,r}}{q_{T,r}\\lambda}\\right). \\] If $q_{T,r}=0$, the rank bound forces $\\Delta_{T,r}=0$ and the tail sum is empty. This proves~\\eqref{eq:spectral-tail-split}; the $r_T=0$ case similarly uses only the tail term.',
    'Because $\\Amat_t^\\top\\Amat_t=\\bI_d$, all $d$ singular values of the $(d+n_t)\\times d$ matrix $\\Amat_t$ equal one. Singular-value perturbation and~\\eqref{eq:stacked-identities} give, for $j=1,\\ldots,d$, \\[ |\\sigma_j(\\Bmat_t)-1| \\le\\|\\Bmat_t-\\Amat_t\\|_{\\rm op}=\\chi_t. \\] For $\\chi_t<1$, every singular value of $\\Bmat_t$ therefore lies in $[1-\\chi_t,1+\\chi_t]$. Squaring the singular values and using $\\Bmat_t^\\top\\Bmat_t=\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\bC_t\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}$ yields $(1-\\chi_t)^2\\bI\\preceq \\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\bC_t\\bCbar_t^{-1/2} \\preceq(1+\\chi_t)^2\\bI$. Congruence by the principal $\\bCbar_t^{1/2}$ proves~\\eqref{eq:drift-sandwich}. If $0\\le\\chi_t\\le\\bar\\chi_t<1$, then $(1-\\bar\\chi_t)^2\\le(1-\\chi_t)^2$ and $(1+\\chi_t)^2\\le(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$, proving the certificate version.',
    'Because $\\Calg_t\\succ 0$ by construction, conjugate gradients (CG) is guaranteed to converge on the system $\\Calg_t\\bu=\\bg_t(a)$, provided the operator is held \\emph{fixed} across all iterations of the solve (the randomized sketch/subsample is drawn into $\\Omega_t$ before action selection; \\S\\ref{sec:problem}). For each action with $\\bg_t(a)\\ne\\mathbf0$ we run CG to the per-action relative energy-norm error',
    'Before $\\tau_w$, $\\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$ bounds each logarithm in $\\Lamalg_T$ by $\\log(1+G^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2))$. Thereafter, for $g_t(a)\\in U$, inversion of the excitation inequality gives $s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)\\le G^2/(\\lambda+\\kappa_wm_t)$; summing $\\log(1+x)\\le x$ proves \\eqref{eq:growing-window-lambda-main}. Since $S_T\\le TH_T^2$, substitution in \\Cref{thm:regret} proves \\eqref{eq:growing-window-regret-main}. The condition-number bound $\\kappa(\\Calg_t)\\le1+m_tG^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)$ makes CG use $O(\\sqrt{m_t}\\log(1/\\bar\\varepsilon))$ iterations, each replaying $m_t$ samples; summing $Km_t^{3/2}$ proves the cost claim.',
    'Both are pre-action quantities and $Q_t=\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|^2$ exactly. If prediction gradients are $L_g$-Lipschitz on the certified region, set',
    'By the matrix determinant lemma applied to $\\Calg_t^{+}=\\Calg_t+\\sigma^{-2}\\bh_t\\bh_t^\\top$,',
    'Conditional sub-Gaussianity gives \\[ \\Pr\\!\\left(|\\eta_s|> \\sigma\\sqrt{2\\log(2T/\\delta_R)}\\mid\\cG_s\\right) \\le\\delta_R/T. \\] Taking expectations and a union bound over $s=1,\\ldots,T$ yields an event of probability at least $1-\\delta_R$ on which this bound holds for every noise term. On that event, $c_s=\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(x_s,a_s)-\\mu^*(x_s,a_s)-\\eta_s$ and $(a+b)^2\\le2a^2+2b^2$ give \\[ c_s^2\\le 2(2B_\\mu)^2+2\\sigma^2\\,2\\log(2T/\\delta_R) =8B_\\mu^2+4\\sigma^2\\log(2T/\\delta_R). \\] Summing the first $t-1$ terms proves~\\eqref{eq:bounded-output-residual}; at $t=1$ both sides are zero.',
    'Consequently, $W=\\Omega(T)$ with a sufficiently large problem-dependent constant keeps $D_{T,W}=O(1)$. For fixed $d$ and fixed problem constants, \\[ R_T=O(d\\sqrt T\\log T)+O(\\sqrt T). \\] This conclusion requires no $D_t<1$ condition. Here $W$ is an abstract near-linearity scale, not generic network width or parameter count, and the result is not an unrestricted neural-training theorem. No optimizer-residual rate is required because the corrected center is used.',
    'Consequently, if a predictable certificate satisfies $\\chi_t\\le\\bar\\chi_t<1$, then $(1-\\bar\\chi_t)^2\\bCbar_t\\preceq\\bC_t \\preceq(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2\\bCbar_t$.',
    'Exact current curvature has $\\kappa_{-,t}=\\kappa_{+,t}=1$. More generally, the operational relative-error certificate',
    'Extend $Q_U$ to an orthogonal basis of $\\R^d$. In that basis $\\bC_t$ is block diagonal with the $r_U\\times r_U$ matrix in \\eqref{eq:projected-width} and $\\lambda I_{d-r_U}$; the query has zero coordinates in the second block. This proves \\eqref{eq:projected-width}, and the same argument proves the frozen-Gram identity. \\Cref{lem:rank-logdet} gives $\\gamma_T\\le\\Gamma_{T,r}$. Every occurrence of $\\gamma_T$ in \\Cref{cor:near-linear} is monotone, so direct substitution gives the display. Under the stated near-linearity scaling, $x_T$, the centering terms, and the linearization terms are bounded, while $\\Gamma_{T,r}=O(r\\log T)$. Hence $\\Omega_{T,r}=O(\\sqrt{r\\log T}+1)$ and the leading product is $O(r\\sqrt T\\log T)$.',
    "Finally let $d_{s,t}=\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|$. The collection residual $c_s=\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(z_s)-r_s$ gives $\\bar r_{s,t}=c_s+\\bg_s^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s)$. The triangle and Cauchy--Schwarz inequalities therefore yield $\\|\\bar{\\mathbf r}_t\\|_2\\le\\sqrt{R_t^{\\rm col}}+G\\sqrt{Q_t}$. Taylor's theorem gives $|e_{s,t}|\\le(L_g/2)d_{s,t}^2$; because both parameters lie in the radius-$R$ region, $d_{s,t}\\le2R$, hence $|e_{s,t}|\\le L_gR d_{s,t}$ and $\\|\\mathbf e_t\\|_2\\le L_gR\\sqrt{Q_t}$. Applying the triangle inequality to $\\bv_t=\\operatorname{Diag}(\\mathbf c_t-\\mathbf{1})\\bar{\\mathbf r}_t +\\operatorname{Diag}(\\mathbf c_t)\\mathbf e_t$, using $\\|\\mathbf c_t-\\mathbf{1}\\|_\\infty=\\rho_t$ and $\\|\\mathbf c_t\\|_\\infty\\le1+\\rho_t$, proves \\eqref{eq:relative-link-v-bound}. Combine this with \\Cref{lem:primitive-pred} and~\\eqref{eq:relative-link-M} to obtain \\eqref{eq:relative-link-psi}.",
    'Fix $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Let $\\rho_s^\\circ$ be the Taylor remainder of $\\mu_{\\btheta^\\circ}$ around $\\btheta_s$, and supply envelopes',
    'Fix a round~$t$ and an action~$a$ with $\\bg:=\\bg_t(a)\\ne\\mathbf0$. After $I_{t,a}$~iterations of CG on the SPD system $\\Calg_t\\bu=\\bg$ (starting from $\\tilde\\bu_0=\\mathbf{0}$), the standard energy-norm bound gives \\[ \\varepsilon_{t,a} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\|\\tilde\\bu_{I_{t,a}} - \\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}} {\\|\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}} \\;\\le\\; 2\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}-1}{\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}+1}\\right)^{\\!I_{t,a}}, \\] where $\\kappa_t=\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Calg_t)/\\lambda_{\\min}(\\Calg_t)$ is the true condition number. The bound is right-hand-side independent, so a common iteration budget bounds $\\max_a\\varepsilon_{t,a}$. This zero-start form is what the fixed-budget certificate~\\eqref{eq:cg-budget} uses. For a \\emph{warm start} $\\tilde\\bu_0\\ne\\mathbf0$, energy-norm minimization over the shifted Krylov space instead gives \\[ \\frac{\\|\\tilde\\bu_{I_{t,a}}-\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}}{\\|\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}} \\;\\le\\; 2\\,q^{\\,I_{t,a}}\\, \\frac{\\|\\tilde\\bu_0-\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}}{\\|\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}}, \\qquad q=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}-1}{\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}+1}, \\] i.e.\\ the extra factor is the initial relative energy error $\\|e_0\\|_{\\Calg_t}/ \\|\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg\\|_{\\Calg_t}$ (equal to one at $\\tilde\\bu_0=\\mathbf0$, recovering the zero-start bound). Under the same certified condition-number bound, adaptive residual stopping (line~\\ref{line:cg}) remains valid with a warm start because it certifies the realized residual directly; but the fixed-budget count~\\eqref{eq:cg-budget} is valid \\emph{as written} only for zero initialization, and a fixed-budget warm-start certificate additionally requires a bound on that initial relative energy error (the zero-start iteration count does not by itself certify a warm-started solve). Any certified upper bound $\\bar\\kappa_t\\ge\\kappa_t$ may replace $\\kappa_t$; for a nonnegative weighted outer-product operator, $\\lambda_{\\min}(\\Calg_t)\\ge\\lambda$ and $\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Calg_t)\\le\\lambda+W_tG^2/\\sigma^2$ (where $W_t=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}w_s$) give \\[ \\kappa_t\\le\\frac{\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Calg_t)}{\\lambda} \\le1+\\frac{W_tG^2}{\\lambda\\sigma^2}=: \\bar\\kappa_t, \\] whereas a general SPD operator requires a separately certified $\\bar\\kappa_t$.',
    'Fix a uniform CG certificate $\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\le\\bar\\varepsilon<1$, let $\\alpha_I=\\sqrt{(1+\\bar\\varepsilon)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon)}$, and suppose $\\zeta_t\\le\\zeta_0/\\sqrt W$ and $R_t^{\\rm col}\\le C_RtP_T$ for $P_T\\ge1$. Fix a rank index $r$ and supply a predictable prefix information envelope $\\mathcal G_{n,r}\\ge\\gamma_n$; write $\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{n,r}=\\max_{j\\le n}\\mathcal G_{j,r}$. The envelope may be the exact-rank bound or either valid predictable spectral-tail schedule in \\Cref{cor:approx-rank-current-ggn}. Define',
    'For $D_t=\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}(r_s-\\mu_{\\btheta_t}) \\nabla^2\\mu_{\\btheta_t}$, the triangle inequality gives $\\|D_t\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\zeta\\lambda$, proving~(i). Combining $-\\|D_t\\|\\bI\\preceq-D_t\\preceq\\|D_t\\|\\bI$ with $\\bC_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$ proves~(ii).',
    'For $\\bg_t(a)=\\mathbf0$ all three terms vanish and the bound is trivial. Otherwise \\Cref{lem:cg} with $\\varepsilon_{t,a}\\le\\bar\\varepsilon_t$ gives $(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)\\le\\tilde s_t^2(a)\\le (1+\\bar\\varepsilon_t)s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)$. The lower bound and~$\\omega_t\\ge0$ give $\\sqrt{\\tilde s_t^2(a)}\\ge\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a)$, so $w_t(a)\\ge\\omega_t\\sqrt{u_t(a)}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a)$; then~\\eqref{eq:transfer} gives $\\sqrt{u_t(a)}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a)\\ge\\bar s_t(a)$, i.e.\\ $w_t(a)\\ge\\omega_t\\bar s_t(a)$. The upper bound gives $\\sqrt{\\tilde s_t^2(a)}\\le\\sqrt{1+\\bar\\varepsilon_t}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a)$, whence $w_t(a)\\le\\omega_t\\sqrt{u_t(a)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)}\\sqrt{1+\\bar\\varepsilon_t}\\, s_{\\Calg,t}(a)=\\alpha_t\\omega_t\\sqrt{u_t(a)}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a)$. Both bounds are per-round; no horizon maximum is taken before summing.',
    'For $\\bg_t(a)=\\mathbf0$ the algorithm sets $\\tilde{s}_t^2(a)=0=s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)$ and~\\eqref{eq:cg-quad} holds trivially.',
    'For $\\tau_w=1$, more explicitly, $L_q(T;1)\\le(G^2/\\sigma^2)[\\lambda^{-1}+\\kappa_w^{-1}h_q(T)]$, where, for $T\\ge2$, $h_q(T)=1+((T-1)^{1-q}-1)/(1-q)$ for $q<1$ and $h_1(T)=1+\\log(T-1)$, while $h_q(1):=0$ in both cases. Writing $r_U=\\dim U$, the excitation premise can first hold nontrivially only once $m_t\\ge r_U$, because $\\operatorname{rank}(\\Calg_t-\\lambda I)\\le m_t$; thus $\\tau_w$ includes the required rank-$r_U$ burn-in. If $H_T=\\max_{t\\le T}\\alpha_t\\sqrt{u_t(a_t)}\\,\\omega_t$, then',
    "For $h_W(q)=\\sqrt W\\tanh(q/\\sqrt W)$, $h_W'(q)=\\operatorname{sech}^2(q/\\sqrt W)$ and \\[ h_W''(q)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt W}\\tanh(q/\\sqrt W) \\operatorname{sech}^2(q/\\sqrt W). \\] The maximum of $2y(1-y^2)$ on $y\\in[0,1]$ is $4/(3\\sqrt3)$, proving the gradient and Hessian bounds in~\\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-constants}; also $\\sqrt W|\\tanh(q/\\sqrt W)|\\le|q|\\le B_\\phi R$. Moreover \\[ \\left|\\frac{d}{dq}\\log h_W'(q)\\right| =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt W}|\\tanh(q/\\sqrt W)|\\le\\frac2{\\sqrt W}. \\] Since $|q_{s,t}-q_s|\\le B_\\phi\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|\\le2B_\\phi R$, $|\\log c_{s,t}|\\le4B_\\phi R/\\sqrt W$. Hence $|c_{s,t}-1|\\le e^{4B_\\phi R/\\sqrt W}-1=\\rho_W$, and solving $e^{4B_\\phi R/\\sqrt W}-1<1$ gives \\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-rho-threshold} for this exponential certificate.",
    'For $r_{\\rm end}=0$, the first term is defined as zero.',
    'For \\Cref{thm:regret} to give a sublinear guarantee, we require the cumulative linearization error $E_T:=\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\epslin(t) = o(T)$; see \\Cref{thm:regret} for the precise dependence. Concretely, $E_T=O(1)$ when $\\|\\btheta^*{-}\\btheta_t\\|=O(1/t)$ (since $\\epslin(t)=O(1/t^2)$ and $\\sum t^{-2}<\\infty$; achievable in convex or strongly convex regimes), and $E_T=O(\\sqrt{T})$ when $\\|\\btheta^*{-}\\btheta_t\\|=O(t^{-1/4})$. Restricting fine-tuning or using a trust region can limit Taylor error, but does not by itself establish $E_T=o(T)$. A frozen backbone with an exactly linear head has zero Taylor remainder; other cases require an explicit cumulative-error bound. These rate examples are illustrative only. The present paper does not prove convergence of the nonconvex optimization path $\\{\\btheta_t\\}$ to~$\\btheta^*$; a sublinear guarantee from this bound therefore requires this term to be controlled by an external stability or convergence argument.',
    'For a fixed-feature window of length $m$, after warm-up each deletion has rank one. Writing $N_{\\rm drop}=\\max\\{0,T-m\\}$, Sherman--Morrison gives the following exact charge for each $t>m$. Let $v_t^{\\rm drop}=\\sigma^{-1}\\bg_{t-m}(\\bx_{t-m},a_{t-m})$ and let $D_t=\\Calg_t^+-v_t^{\\rm drop}(v_t^{\\rm drop})^\\top=\\Calg_{t+1}$ be the remaining-window matrix. Then $-\\log\\det(\\bI+\\Xi_t)= \\log[1+(v_t^{\\rm drop})^\\top D_t^{-1}v_t^{\\rm drop}]$, and therefore \\[ \\Valg_T\\le N_{\\rm drop}\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{G^2}{\\lambda\\sigma^2}\\right). \\] This can be linear in $T$. A relinearized window need not be a rank-one replacement. Likewise, merely holding a stale operator fixed does not make non-refresh transitions canonical rank-one updates, so geometric refresh timing alone does not imply a logarithmic charge.',
    'For a known radius-$R$ trust region, the final numerator term may instead be bounded by $L_g^2RQ_t$. Finally, for an $L_\\mu$-Lipschitz prediction gradient define $\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}=\\frac{L_\\mu}{2}(S+\\|\\btheta_t\\|)^2$, $\\bar F_t=\\sum_{s<t}(\\bar\\epsilon_s^{\\rm lin})^2$, and',
    'For an \\emph{unrescaled current-parameter window} (or any current-parameter downweighted subset with weights in $[0,1]$), \\Cref{prop:window} gives the \\emph{deterministic} upper factor $\\kappa_{+,t}=1$ with \\emph{no} distributional assumption, so \\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion} applies directly with $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$. Only the \\emph{stale periodic-refresh} operator (\\S\\ref{sec:curvop}(iii)), which reuses Jacobians from an older parameter, lacks a deterministic $\\kappa_{+,t}$; there \\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion} provides a guarantee only when a one-sided bound $\\hat\\bC_t\\preceq\\kappa_{+,t}\\bC_t$ is separately certified.',
    'For an integer $0\\le r\\le d$, define',
    'For explicit targets $\\bar x_{\\rm drift}\\in(0,1)$ and $\\Psi,b_F,\\eta_E>0$, the sufficient conditions',
    'For squared loss, the corrected center makes this chain independent of nonlinear optimizer convergence. The representation may follow any predictable path inside the certified region, while historical error, current bias, transport, operator distortion, solver accuracy, and approximate score maximization remain explicit charges. The construction reduces to the usual LinUCB determinant-potential argument for frozen linear features and exact solves. Under the assumptions of \\Cref{cor:global-near-linear-new}, for fixed $d$ and fixed problem constants, taking $W=\\Omega(T)$ with a sufficiently large problem-dependent constant yields $R_T=O(d\\sqrt T\\log T)+O(\\sqrt T)$ without requiring $D_t<1$. Here $W$ is an abstract smoothness scale, not generic network width.',
    'For the exact-curvature theorem, Algorithm~\\ref{alg:ccucb} is run with $\\mathcal{S}_t=\\{1,\\ldots,t{-}1\\}$, $w_s=1$, full action enumeration, $\\Calg_t=\\bC_t$, and the transfer factor $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ from \\Cref{lem:whitened}. For an approximate operator $\\Calg_t=\\hat\\bC_t$, use $u_t=\\kappa_{+,t}(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ from \\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion}. The selection rule needs only the \\emph{one-sided} upper transfer factor~$u_t(a)$ for optimism; no lower spectral factor is required (the cost of the operator is charged through the realized dynamic complexity $\\Lamalg_T$). Algorithm~\\ref{alg:ccucb-detailed} computes $u_t$, $\\bar\\psi_t$, and $\\bar\\beta_t$ from pre-action state. The operator-specific inputs are the proved factor $\\kappa_{+,t}$ and condition upper bound $\\bar\\kappa_t$; exact full and unrescaled-subset operators use $\\kappa_{+,t}=1$. Windowed/refresh curvature and action screening are practical heuristics unless the corresponding one-sided transfer factor or optimal-arm-survival condition is separately verified.',
    'For the growing window of \\Cref{thm:growing-window}, one solve costs $O(m_t^{3/2}\\log(1/\\bar\\varepsilon))$ sample-CVPs under the analytic condition-number bound. The following table uses the sufficient fixed-burn-in setting $\\tau_w=O(1)$, $H_T=\\widetilde O(1)$, and $E_T=\\widetilde O(T^{1-q/2})$; $E_T=o(T)$ alone is insufficient. The sample-CVP column counts only width solves. With uniformly bounded full-batch optimizer/certificate passes, the final column adds the $T^2$ full-history training term. These are achieved upper bounds for this implementation, not a Pareto frontier, lower bound, or wall-clock measurement (logarithmic factors suppressed): \\input{tables/growing_window_pareto.tex}',
    'For the second inequality, use $\\tau_T-\\Delta_{T,r}\\le\\tau_T\\le TG^2/\\sigma^2$ in the head term and $q\\log(1+x/q)\\le x$ with $x=\\Delta_{T,r}/\\lambda$ in the tail term. This proves~\\eqref{eq:spectral-tail-logdet} without evaluating a zero denominator. The stated edge cases follow immediately.',
    'From~\\eqref{eq:pseudo-response}, $y_s = \\bg_s^\\top\\btheta^* + \\rho_s + \\eta_s$ with $|\\rho_s|\\le\\epslin(s)$. The closed form of $\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$ gives \\[ \\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}} - \\btheta^* = -\\lambda\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\btheta^* + \\bCbar_t^{-1}\\sigma^{-2}\\!\\sum_{s<t}\\bg_s\\rho_s + \\bCbar_t^{-1}\\sigma^{-2}\\!\\sum_{s<t}\\bg_s\\eta_s. \\] Taking the inner product with~$\\bg_t(a)$ and bounding in absolute value:',
    'Furthermore $\\bar F_{T+1}\\le4c_h^2R^4T/W$ and $\\bar E_T\\le2c_hR^2T/\\sqrt W$. Hence, if $\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{T,r}=O(r\\log T)$ with fixed problem, confidence, and CG constants, \\[ R_T=O(r\\sqrt T\\log T)+O(T/\\sqrt W). \\] At $W=\\Theta(TP_T)$ this is $O(r\\sqrt T\\log T+\\sqrt{T/P_T})$, and therefore $O(r\\sqrt T\\log T+\\sqrt T)$ up to the displayed residual factor.',
    'Here $\\lambda>0$ and $\\sigma>0$. Define the corresponding widths',
    'If $H_T=\\widetilde O(1)$, $E_T=\\widetilde O(T^{1-q/2})$, and the burn-in obeys $\\tau_w=\\widetilde O(T^{1-q})$ for $q<1$ (or $\\tau_w=\\widetilde O(1)$ for $q=1$), then \\eqref{eq:growing-window-regret-main} simplifies to $R_T=\\widetilde O(T^{1-q/2})$; otherwise its displayed $L_q$ and $E_T$ terms remain. This is conditional on the fixed subspace, excitation, and all confidence, transfer, optimizer, and CG certificates above. Certified worst-case \\emph{width-solve} work is $O(KT^{1+3q/2}\\log(1/\\bar\\varepsilon))$ sample-CVPs when a fixed uniform target $\\bar\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$ is enforced; this count excludes model optimization, candidate-gradient computation, and certificate construction.',
    'If $\\rho_W<1$, exact current curvature with the original nonlinear center and the relative certificate of \\Cref{lem:relative-link-drift} satisfies',
    'If $\\rho_t<1$, it also satisfies',
    'If $\\zeta < 1$, then $(1{-}\\zeta)\\bC_t \\preceq \\bH_t \\preceq (1{+}\\zeta)\\bC_t$.',
    'If $\\|\\btheta_s\\|,\\|\\btheta_t\\|\\le R$, the final term in \\eqref{eq:path-M} may be replaced by $L_g^2RQ_t$. If a nonnegative $\\cHist_t$-measurable optimizer certificate satisfies $\\|\\nabla\\cL_t(\\btheta_t)\\|\\le\\zeta_t$, then \\[ \\bar\\psi_t=(\\zeta_t+\\bar M_t)/\\sqrt\\lambda\\ge\\psi_t. \\] If prediction gradients are $L_\\mu$-Lipschitz on a convex region containing $\\btheta_t$ and $\\btheta^*$ and $\\|\\btheta^*\\|\\le S$, then \\[ \\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}:=\\frac{L_\\mu}{2}(S+\\|\\btheta_t\\|)^2 \\ge\\epslin(t). \\] Consequently $\\bar F_t=\\sum_{s<t}(\\bar\\epsilon_s^{\\rm lin})^2\\ge F_t$ and $\\bar E_T=\\sum_{t\\le T}\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}\\ge E_T$.',
    'If $x_T<1$, the predictable radius',
    'If additionally $\\Calg_{T+1}=\\lambda\\bI+A$, where $A\\succeq0$, $\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le r_{\\rm end}$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(A)\\le W_{\\rm end}G^2/\\sigma^2$ for a specified total endpoint weight $W_{\\rm end}\\ge0$, then, for $r_{\\rm end}>0$,',
    'In addition, \\eqref{eq:near-linear-rates-main} holds and \\[ \\bar\\psi_t\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt\\lambda}\\!\\left\\{ \\frac{\\zeta_0}{\\sqrt W}+\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\\!\\left[ \\frac{2c_gR\\sqrt{C_Rt(t-1)P_T}+6Gc_gR^2(t-1)}{\\sqrt W} +\\frac{4c_g^2R^3(t-1)}{W}\\right]\\right\\}. \\]',
    'In frozen linear-head models $Q_t$ may be nonzero while $L_g=L_\\mu=0$; then $\\bar\\chi_t=\\bar M_t=\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}=0$, and exact optimization also gives $\\bar\\psi_t=0$.',
    'In particular, bounded transfer is ensured by $W>4c_g^2R^2(T-1)/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)=\\Omega(T)$ up to problem constants, and $E_T\\le2c_\\mu R^2T/\\sqrt W$. If $\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{T,r}=O(r\\log T)$---for example, if $\\max_{n\\le T}\\bar\\Delta_{n,r}=O(r\\log T)$ at fixed $\\lambda$---and $W=\\Omega(T)$ with a sufficiently large constant to keep $x_T$ bounded away from one, then \\[ R_T=O(r\\sqrt T\\log T)+O(\\sqrt T) \\] up to confidence and CG constants.',
    'In particular, the bound is sublinear whenever $\\Lamalg_T S_T=o(T^2)$ and $E_T=o(T)$. The exact-curvature instantiation is $\\Calg_t=\\bC_t$ with $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ from \\Cref{lem:whitened}; approximate operators are covered through \\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion}. The proof is in \\Cref{app:proof}.',
    'In the convex last-layer regime, under exact optimization, $\\btheta_t = \\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$ and $\\bar\\psi_t=0$. The full prediction error at $(\\bx_t,a)$ is bounded by the triangle inequality, with the centering discrepancy \\emph{action-scaled}:',
    'In the regret bound (\\Cref{thm:regret}), the CG inflation enters through the per-round factor $\\alpha_t = \\sqrt{(1{+}\\bar\\varepsilon_t)/(1{-}\\bar\\varepsilon_t)}$, inside the sum of squares $\\sum_t\\alpha_t^2 u_t(a_t)\\omega_t^2$; no horizon maximum over $\\bar\\varepsilon_t$ is taken.',
    "Krylov methods for Hessian spectral analysis have been run on networks with $>10^8$ parameters under distributed training \\citep{granziol2026hessian}, which shows that related curvature--vector primitives can be implemented at that scale. This does not establish feasibility for the exact per-example GGN JVP/VJP path analyzed here, nor does it measure end-to-end bandit training. Warm-starting CG from the previous round's solution can reduce the iteration count in practice.",
    'Let $\\bu=\\Calg^{-1}\\bq$. Since $s_{\\Calg}^2=\\bq^\\top\\bu=\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg}^2$, \\[ \\left|\\bq^\\top\\tilde\\bu-s_{\\Calg}^2\\right| =\\left|\\langle\\bu,\\tilde\\bu-\\bu\\rangle_{\\Calg}\\right| \\le\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg}\\|\\tilde\\bu-\\bu\\|_{\\Calg} \\le\\varepsilon s_{\\Calg}^2. \\] This proves the pointwise two-sided quadratic-form comparison. Dividing its lower side by $1-\\varepsilon$ gives upper validity for this query, while combining both sides gives the displayed sharpness factor. Applying the lower inequality actionwise on the simultaneous family event gives the all-domain upper condition; only the played query uses the upper inequality for sharpness. For PCG the argument is unchanged once the certificate is expressed in the original $\\Calg$-energy norm.',
    'Let $\\mathcal J$ be a finite or countable certificate-source index set. For $j\\in\\mathcal J$, choose an $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable failure budget $\\delta_{j,t}$ before drawing its random object. Let $\\mathcal P_{j,t}$ be the pre-reward sigma-algebra immediately before that draw. It may equal $\\cHist_t^-$ for a round-start draw or include the selected action and earlier randomness for a post-selection check. It is sufficient that \\[ \\Pr(\\mathcal E_{j,t}^c\\mid\\mathcal P_{j,t})\\le\\delta_{j,t}, \\qquad \\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\sum_{t=1}^T\\delta_{j,t} \\le\\delta_{\\rm cert}\\quad\\text{almost surely}. \\] The tower property and a union bound give simultaneous validity at least $1-\\delta_{\\rm cert}$ without independence. Countability makes the joint event measurable, the nonnegative double sum well-defined, and the union bound valid. On an uncountable score domain, the solver upper event must already be simultaneous. A pointwise certificate cannot be union-bounded after adaptive selection unless it is separately verified at the selected action with the correct conditioning. Realized terminal spectral quantities may be used for post-hoc analysis, but they cannot be inserted into an earlier action-time radius unless a predictable prefix envelope was supplied at that time.',
    'Let $\\xi_{t,i}$ be the eigenvalues of $\\Xi_t$. At most $r_t$ are negative, each is at least $-\\nu_t$, and every nonnegative eigenvalue makes a nonpositive contribution to $-\\sum_i\\log(1+\\xi_{t,i})$. Hence $[-\\log\\det(\\bI+\\Xi_t)]_+\\le r_t\\log(1/(1-\\nu_t))$, proving \\eqref{eq:rank-refresh}. List the nonzero eigenvalues of $A$ and pad with zeros to obtain $a_1,\\ldots,a_{r_{\\rm end}}$. Concavity gives \\[ \\sum_i\\log(1+a_i/\\lambda) \\le r_{\\rm end}\\log\\!\\left(1+ \\frac{\\sum_i a_i}{r_{\\rm end}\\lambda}\\right), \\] and the trace premise plus \\Cref{lem:dynamic-potential} proves \\eqref{eq:endpoint-rank}.',
    'Let $c_t=\\beta_t[1+\\alpha_te^{\\bar D_t} \\sqrt{\\kappa_{+,t}/\\kappa_{-,t}}]$. Summation and one Cauchy--Schwarz application give \\[ R_T-2\\sum_tb_t(a_t)-\\sum_t\\xi_t \\le\\sqrt{\\sum_tc_t^2}\\sqrt{\\sum_t\\bar s_t^2(a_t)}. \\] Since $\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\le G^2/\\lambda$, the scalar inequality $x\\le(1+M)\\log(1+x)$ for $0\\le x\\le M$, with $x=\\sigma^{-2}\\bar s_t^2(a_t)$ and $M=G^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)$, yields',
    'More explicitly, choose a fixed target $\\rho_0\\in(0,1)$ and suppose',
    'More generally, suppose there is a measurable function $\\nu_t:[0,1]\\to[0,\\infty)$ with $\\nu_t\\in L^1([0,1])$ such that \\[ \\dot\\Bmat(\\tau)^\\top\\dot\\Bmat(\\tau) \\preceq\\nu_t(\\tau)^2\\bV(\\tau) \\] almost everywhere. Then $\\mathcal D(\\bV)\\le2\\int_0^1\\nu_t(\\tau)d\\tau$. For nonnegative $\\nu_t$, the displayed PSD premise is equivalently the norm condition $\\|\\dot\\Bmat(\\tau)\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\nu_t(\\tau)$ almost everywhere.',
    'No exact tangent subspace is assumed. A small spectral tail can contain decision-relevant directions and does not supply their basis, so full-dimensional CG may still be necessary. Exact rank is recovered when $\\Delta_{T,r}=0$; the supplied-subspace result \\Cref{cor:rank-near-linear} is then an additional computational special case. The corrected-center version again replaces $\\omega_t$ by $\\bar\\beta_t$.',
    'O(\\tau_w+T^{1-q}),',
    'Operationally, if before round $t$ a supplied deterministic or $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable envelope $\\bar\\Delta_{t-1,r}$ upper-bounds the spectral tail of $\\bCbar_t-\\lambda\\bI$, the old predictable envelope below remains valid: \\[',
    'Positive definiteness of both~$\\bC_t$ and~$\\bCbar_t$ follows from $\\lambda>0$ and the outer-product form; it is not an independent assumption. All theoretical results hold under these explicit assumptions; we do \\emph{not} claim a regret guarantee for the full nonlinear model outside the local-linearization regime.',
    'Python 3.12 with `numpy` and `scipy` (`experiments/requirements.txt` records the versions the study declared: NumPy 2.2.3, SciPy 1.15.2). `pytest` is needed only for the test suite. Nothing else is required, and nothing downloads anything.',
    'Set $\\gamma_0=0$, and define',
    'Set $\\hat s=\\sqrt{U}$. If $L>0$, the observable factor $\\alpha=\\sqrt{U/L}$ verifies played-action sharpness. If $L\\le0<U$, the interval supplies no finite positive multiplicative sharpness factor; the solve must continue or an additive-width theorem must be used. If $U=0$, the exact inverse quadratic form is zero and the solver returns $\\hat s=0$. For $\\bq=0$, skip the solve and return zero.',
    'Since $\\sup_u|2\\operatorname{sech}^2(u)\\tanh(u)|=4/(3\\sqrt3)$, \\Cref{cor:global-near-linear-new} applies with \\[ G=B_\\phi,\\qquad c_\\mu=c_g=c_h:=\\frac{4B_\\phi^2}{3\\sqrt3}. \\] The symbol $W$ is a smoothness scale here; it is not identified with hidden width or parameter count.',
    'Substitute $\\delta_{s,t}=(c_{s,t}-1)\\bg_s$ in the definition of $M_t$: \\[ \\bar r_{s,t}\\delta_{s,t}+e_{s,t}\\bg_s+e_{s,t}\\delta_{s,t} =\\{\\bar r_{s,t}(c_{s,t}-1)+c_{s,t}e_{s,t}\\}\\bg_s. \\] This proves the first identity in~\\eqref{eq:relative-link-M}. Left multiplication by $\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}$ gives the transformed identity $\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}M_t=\\sigma^{-1}\\Gmat_t\\bv_t$, so \\[ \\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} =\\sigma^{-1}\\|\\Gmat_t\\bv_t\\|_2 \\le\\sigma^{-1}\\|\\bv_t\\|_2, \\] which proves the second identity and uses equivalently $\\Gmat_t^\\top\\Gmat_t\\preceq I_{t-1}$.',
    'Substitute $\\epslin(t)=0$, $\\bar\\psi_t=0$, $u_t(a)=1$, and $\\Lamalg_T=\\gamma_T$, $\\Valg_T=0$ into~\\eqref{eq:regret-bound}; the frozen Gram is monotone, so $\\Xi_t=0$ and the dynamic potential collapses to the information gain $\\gamma_T$. The LinUCB form is \\Cref{cor:worstcase} with $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_I$, $u_t=1$, $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t\\le\\bar\\beta_{\\max,T}$.',
    "The $\\{Y_s\\}_{s=1}^m$ are fixed PSD self-adjoint matrices (deterministic conditional on the history $\\cF_{t-1}$ and the current parameter~$\\btheta_t$). The displayed proof is for \\emph{with-replacement} (i.i.d.) sampling. The without-replacement version follows from the domination argument of \\citet{gross2010note}, building on Hoeffding's reduction \\citep{hoeffding1963probability}: the matrix moment-generating function of a sum sampled without replacement from a fixed finite collection of self-adjoint matrices is dominated by that of the corresponding i.i.d.\\ sum, so every tail bound derived from the matrix Laplace-transform method, including the matrix Bernstein inequality of \\citet[Thm.~6.1.1]{tropp2015introduction}, carries over with the same constants. In our setting the $\\{Y_s\\}$ are \\emph{fixed} PSD matrices (deterministic conditional on $\\cF_{t-1}$ and~$\\btheta_t$) and only the index set $\\mathcal{S}_t$ is random, so the domination argument applies directly. We therefore state and prove the bound for i.i.d.\\ draws $s_1,\\dots,s_n$ uniform on~$\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$; the without-replacement guarantee is at least as strong. Define the centered random matrices $Z_k = (m/n)Y_{s_k} - V/n$ (mean zero, independent). The range satisfies $\\|Z_k\\|_{\\mathrm{op}} \\le (m/n)B + (1/n)\\|V\\| \\le 2mB/n =: R$ (using $\\|V\\|\\le mB$). For the variance parameter, since $Y_s\\preceq B\\bI$, \\[ \\mathbb{E}[Z_k Z_k^\\top] \\;\\preceq\\; \\tfrac{m^2}{n^2}\\mathbb{E}[Y_{s_1}^2] \\;=\\; \\tfrac{m}{n^2}\\sum_{s=1}^m Y_s^2 \\;\\preceq\\; \\tfrac{m}{n^2}B\\,V \\;\\preceq\\; \\tfrac{m^2B^2}{n^2}\\bI, \\] so $v := \\bigl\\|\\sum_{k=1}^n\\mathbb{E}[Z_kZ_k^\\top]\\bigr\\|\\le m^2B^2/n$. By the matrix Bernstein inequality \\citep[Thm.~6.1.1]{tropp2015introduction}, $\\Pr[\\|\\hat V{-}V\\| \\ge t] \\le 2d\\exp\\!\\bigl(-t^2/(2v + 2Rt/3)\\bigr)$. Choosing $t_0=\\sqrt{2vL}+\\tfrac{2}{3}RL$ makes the matrix-Bernstein tail probability at most~$\\delta$. Substituting $v\\le m^2B^2/n$ and $R=2mB/n$ gives $t_0\\le mB\\sqrt{2L/n}+\\tfrac{4mBL}{3n}$, yielding~\\eqref{eq:subsample-dev}. For~\\eqref{eq:subsample-n}, require each summand in~\\eqref{eq:subsample-dev} to be $\\le\\varepsilon\\lambda/2$: $mB\\sqrt{2L/n}\\le\\varepsilon\\lambda/2$ gives $n\\ge 8m^2B^2L/(\\varepsilon^2\\lambda^2)$, and $4mBL/(3n)\\le\\varepsilon\\lambda/2$ gives $n\\ge 8mBL/(3\\varepsilon\\lambda)$. Summing the two conditions yields~\\eqref{eq:subsample-n}; this is a conservative sufficient condition (using $a+b$ rather than $\\max\\{a,b\\}$), not a sharp threshold. The multiplicative sandwich follows: $\\|\\hat V{-}V\\|\\le\\varepsilon\\lambda$ gives $\\bC_t - \\varepsilon\\lambda\\bI \\preceq \\hat\\bC_t \\preceq \\bC_t + \\varepsilon\\lambda\\bI$. Since $\\bC_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$, we have $\\varepsilon\\lambda\\bI \\preceq \\varepsilon\\bC_t$, so $\\bC_t - \\varepsilon\\lambda\\bI \\succeq (1{-}\\varepsilon)\\bC_t$ and $\\bC_t + \\varepsilon\\lambda\\bI \\preceq (1{+}\\varepsilon)\\bC_t$.",
    'The CG minimax property gives $\\|e_I\\|_{\\Calg_t}/\\|e_0\\|_{\\Calg_t} \\le\\min_{p\\in\\mathcal P_I,\\,p(0)=1} \\max_{x\\in[\\lambda_{\\min},\\lambda_{\\max}]}|p(x)|$. Choosing the scaled Chebyshev polynomial bounds this maximum by $2[(\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}-1)/(\\sqrt{\\kappa_t}+1)]^I$; this ratio is increasing in $\\kappa_t$, so $\\bar\\kappa_t\\ge\\kappa_t$ proves~\\eqref{eq:cg-rate}. $\\tilde{s}_t^2(a) - s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a) = \\bg^\\top(\\tilde\\bu-\\bu) = (\\Calg_t\\bu)^\\top(\\tilde\\bu-\\bu) = \\bu^\\top\\Calg_t(\\tilde\\bu-\\bu)$. By Cauchy--Schwarz in the $\\Calg_t$-norm, $|\\bu^\\top\\Calg_t(\\tilde\\bu-\\bu)| \\le\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg_t}\\|\\tilde\\bu-\\bu\\|_{\\Calg_t} \\le\\varepsilon_{t,a}\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg_t}^2 = \\varepsilon_{t,a}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)$. For the outer-product special case the condition-number bound follows from $\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Calg_t)\\le\\lambda+\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t} w_s\\|\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\|^2 \\le\\lambda+W_tG^2/\\sigma^2$.',
    'The check that the redaction did not touch the numbers is step 2 below. The published tables, figures and CSVs are regenerated **from the packaged aggregate** and must come out byte-identical to the committed copies. They could not, if the redaction had perturbed anything they depend on.',
    'The controlled scaled-tanh audit separates formal validity from practical usefulness. All 600 transport-Hessian trajectories retained simultaneous reference confidence and transported optimism, and the locked grid recorded no deterministic audit failure. Yet the analytic path envelope was orders of magnitude longer than the selected-path diagnostic, every reported theorem bound was vacuous relative to the deterministic cap, and the three comparison policies had lower sample mean regret at the primary horizon. The result is a negative practical finding about this certificate and environment, not evidence for a uniform ordering of curvature methods.',
    'The discrepancy is thus \\emph{action-scaled}: it multiplies the confidence width $\\bar s_t(a)$ rather than entering additively. The certificate absorbs the same three sources as before---optimizer residual $\\zeta_t$, replayed-Jacobian drift inside $M_t$, and higher-order nonlinear terms---but now inside a single per-round scalar $\\bar\\psi_t$. In the convex-in-parameters regime (frozen backbone with a linear reward head), under exact optimization $\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}=\\btheta_t$ and $\\bar\\psi_t=0$. The corrected-center variant of \\Cref{cor:corrected-center} removes $\\bar\\psi_t$ entirely, at the cost of an explicit frozen-feature solve.',
    'The equality follows by substituting the two constant spectral blocks. For $x\\in[0,1]$, $\\log(1+x)\\ge x/2$; apply this with $x=\\Delta/(q\\lambda)$ and multiply by $q$.',
    'The exact solution is $\\Calg^{-1}\\bq=\\tilde\\bu+\\Calg^{-1}\\br$. Substitute this expression and $\\bq=\\Calg\\tilde\\bu+\\br$ into the quadratic form. The remainder is $\\br^\\top\\Calg^{-1}\\br$, which lies between zero and $\\|\\br\\|_2^2/\\lambda_{\\Calg}$.',
    'The following lemma supplies the exact-curvature transfer factor through a \\emph{whitened, relative} feature-drift bound: the drift is measured in the $\\bCbar_t^{-1}$ metric and accumulated in $\\ell_2$, and the resulting one-sided bound needs \\emph{no} smallness condition.',
    'The historical deterministic term in \\eqref{eq:beta-corrected-new} follows from $\\Phi_t^\\top\\bVbar_t^{-1}\\Phi_t\\preceq I$ for $\\Phi_t=[\\bq_1/\\sigma,\\ldots,\\bq_{t-1}/\\sigma]$. Its scaling is $1/\\sigma$, not $1/\\sigma^2$. Fixed nonvanishing misspecification generally creates a linear current-bias term and can also make the historical radius vacuous. This is a graceful-degradation statement, not a robust misspecified-bandit guarantee.',
    'The linearized ridge objective satisfies $\\nabla \\bar{\\cL}_t(\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}})=0$ and, by its quadratic form, \\[ \\nabla \\bar{\\cL}_t(\\btheta_t) = \\bCbar_t(\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}). \\] Thus $\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}} =\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\nabla\\bar{\\cL}_t(\\btheta_t)$. Using $\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(z_s)-r_s=\\bar r_{s,t}+e_{s,t}$ and $\\bg_{s,t}=\\bg_s+\\delta_{s,t}$, the gradients of the nonlinear loss and the frozen-feature linearized loss satisfy \\[ \\nabla \\cL_t(\\btheta_t) = \\nabla \\bar{\\cL}_t(\\btheta_t)+M_t, \\qquad \\text{so}\\qquad \\nabla \\bar{\\cL}_t(\\btheta_t)=\\nabla \\cL_t(\\btheta_t)-M_t . \\] Therefore, by Cauchy--Schwarz in the $\\bCbar_t$-norm, \\[ |\\bg_t(a)^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}})| \\le \\bar s_t(a) \\|\\nabla \\bar{\\cL}_t(\\btheta_t)\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\le \\bar s_t(a) \\left( \\|\\nabla \\cL_t(\\btheta_t)\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} + \\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\right). \\] Since $\\bCbar_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$, $\\|\\nabla \\cL_t(\\btheta_t)\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}\\le \\zeta_t/\\sqrt{\\lambda}$, which gives the geometric inequality $|\\bg_t(a)^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}})| \\le\\bar s_t(a)\\bigl(\\zeta_t/\\sqrt{\\lambda}+\\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}\\bigr) =\\bar s_t(a)\\,\\psi_t$, i.e.\\ the bound of \\eqref{eq:psi-def}. For the certificate, $\\bCbar_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$ also gives $\\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}\\le\\|M_t\\|_2/\\sqrt{\\lambda}$, so $\\psi_t\\le(\\zeta_t+\\|M_t\\|_2)/\\sqrt{\\lambda}=:\\bar\\psi_t\\ge\\psi_t$, the proposed certificate. For the final display, Lipschitzness gives $\\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|\\le L_g\\Delta_{s,t}$ and the Taylor remainder bound $|e_{s,t}|\\le (L_g/2)\\Delta_{s,t}^2$; applying these together with $\\|\\bg_s\\|\\le G$ (\\Cref{asm:bounded}) and $|\\bar r_{s,t}|\\le B_{t,\\delta}$ term by term in the definition of $M_t$ gives the claimed bound on $\\|M_t\\|_2$.',
    'The locked experiment tests the resulting interfaces in a 29-parameter scaled-tanh model with dense current and frozen matrices and exact Cholesky solves. It is a controlled transport-policy audit, not an approximate-operator or CG benchmark. All declared float64 confidence and optimism diagnostics pass, but the analytic path envelope is highly conservative, the numerical regret bound is vacuous, and all three comparison policies have lower sample mean regret in the reported primary-horizon conditions (\\Cref{sec:transport-instantiation}).',
    'The locked grid contains horizons $T\\in\\{250,500,1000\\}$, 50 evaluation seeds, and target labels $D_{\\rm target}\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1,2\\}$. Each $(T,D_{\\rm target})$ cell is a separately executed model condition because both quantities enter the rule for $W$. The target label is neither realized transport distance nor generic network width, so trends across targets are not a dose response to certificate inflation. The four policies share contexts and potential-noise tables. The primary \\emph{transport Hessian} policy uses $\\bar D_t^Q$ in the score. The \\emph{transport endpoint} policy replaces it by the dense endpoint Thompson distance computed from the learner-known matrices $\\bVbar_t$ and $\\bV_t$ before the reward is observed; it is a dense diagnostic comparator. The \\emph{frozen reference} policy uses the frozen width directly. The \\emph{naive current} policy omits transport and is intentionally uncertified. Each policy has its own selected history and representation path. Ten disjoint tuning seeds selected learning rate $3\\times10^{-4}$ and 20 full-batch steps followed by projection each round by preregistered all-action prediction MSE; evaluation seeds never entered that selection. The environment was not filtered after generation. Within each $T=1000$ target cell, the 50-seed means round to optimal-action entropy $1.579$, optimality gap $0.123$, best-fixed-action regret $180.4$, and online context-free mean-only regret $185.4$. All five actions are optimal somewhere in every evaluation stream. These diagnostics were reported after the grid was fixed; they were not used to select seeds.',
    'The one-sided fallback theorem uses only the bound~\\eqref{eq:onesided-C}, which holds for \\emph{any} $\\chi_t\\ge0$; the smallness condition $\\chi_t<1$ is needed only for the lower half of the two-sided sandwich~\\eqref{eq:drift-sandwich}, which in turn is used by the explicit near-linear corollary and by any optional analysis that explicitly assumes a two-sided sandwich. An unrescaled current-parameter window is handled by \\Cref{prop:window} through a one-sided relation and does not require this condition. The binding quantity is the \\emph{whitened $\\ell_2$ accumulation} $\\chi_t\\le\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_F$ of replayed-Jacobian drift, measured in the $\\bCbar_t^{-1}$ metric---a relative, whitened $\\ell_2$ bound that can be sharper than an $\\ell_1$ sum normalized only by $\\lambda$ when the drift occurs in well-observed directions (it credits directions the design has already explored), though it does not dominate uniformly. Together, \\Cref{lem:primitive-pred,lem:whitened} identify exact whitened diagnostic targets. The operational upper bounds in \\Cref{lem:path-certificates} replace those targets by the predictable path statistics $Q_t$ and $R_t$, the certified optimizer residual $\\zeta_t$, and analytic smoothness constants. Thus $\\bar\\psi_t$ and $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ require only $O(d)$ additional state; the exact whitened quantities remain post-hoc diagnostics used to measure the conservatism of these certificates.',
    "The parallel-axis identity follows after writing $\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s=(\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta_{<t}) +(\\bar\\btheta_{<t}-\\btheta_s)$; the cross term sums to zero. \\Cref{lem:whitened} and $\\|\\nabla\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(z_s)-\\nabla\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(z_s)\\| \\le L_g\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|$ give \\eqref{eq:path-chi}. Let $d_{s,t}=\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|$. In \\Cref{lem:primitive-pred}, \\[ |\\bar r_{s,t}|\\le |c_s|+Gd_{s,t},\\qquad \\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|\\le L_gd_{s,t},\\qquad |e_{s,t}|\\le(L_g/2)d_{s,t}^2. \\] Thus each summand defining $M_t$ has norm at most \\[ L_g|c_s|d_{s,t}+\\frac{3GL_g}{2}d_{s,t}^2 +\\frac{L_g^2}{2}d_{s,t}^3. \\] Cauchy--Schwarz gives $\\sum|c_s|d_{s,t}\\le\\sqrt{R_t^{\\rm col}Q_t}$, while $\\sum d_{s,t}^2=Q_t$ and $\\sum d_{s,t}^3\\le Q_t^{3/2}$, proving \\eqref{eq:path-M}. Under the trust region, $d_{s,t}\\le2R$, so $\\sum d_{s,t}^3\\le2RQ_t$. The centering claim follows from \\Cref{lem:primitive-pred} and $\\bCbar_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$. Finally Taylor's theorem and $\\|\\btheta^*-\\btheta_t\\|\\le S+\\|\\btheta_t\\|$ prove the remainder, $F_t$, and $E_T$ claims.",
    'The per-round argument in \\Cref{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential} gives \\[ \\Delta_t\\le2H_t^{\\rm gap}\\bar s_t(a_t)+2\\epslin(t). \\] On a suboptimal round, $\\Delta_t\\ge\\Delta_{\\min}$ and hence $2\\epslin(t)\\le\\Delta_t/2$. Rearranging and using $H_t^{\\rm gap}\\le H_{\\max}^{\\rm gap}$ gives $\\Delta_t\\le4H_{\\max}^{\\rm gap}\\bar s_t(a_t)$. Squaring this inequality and using $\\Delta_t\\ge\\Delta_{\\min}$ yields \\[ \\Delta_t\\le\\frac{\\Delta_t^2}{\\Delta_{\\min}} \\le\\frac{16(H_{\\max}^{\\rm gap})^2}{\\Delta_{\\min}}\\bar s_t^2(a_t). \\] Optimal rounds contribute zero. Sum over $t$ and apply $\\sum_t\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\le (\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\gamma_T$ to prove the display; the three substitutions follow from \\Cref{lem:rank-logdet,lem:spectral-tail-logdet}.',
    'The previous additive stacked-design result remains a useful local corollary. If $\\chi_t<1$ and',
    'The proof, filtration-order pseudocode, and a scalar-tanh specialization with $G\\le B_\\phi$ and $L_\\mu=L_g\\le4B_\\phi^2/(3\\sqrt3)$ are in \\Cref{lem:path-certificates,cor:tanh-link}.',
    'The running maximum $\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{n,r}$ is predictable and dominates both $\\mathcal G_{n,r}$ and every earlier prefix envelope. The trust region gives $Q_t\\le4R^2(t-1)$, so \\Cref{lem:path-certificates} yields $\\bar\\chi_t\\le2c_gR\\sqrt{t-1}/(\\sigma\\sqrt{\\lambda W})\\le x_T$, $\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}\\le2c_\\mu R^2/\\sqrt W$, $\\bar F_t\\le4c_\\mu^2R^4(t-1)/W$, and $\\bar E_T\\le2c_\\mu R^2T/\\sqrt W$. Hence \\eqref{eq:corrected-near-linear-beta} dominates \\eqref{eq:beta}. Apply the corrected-center form of \\Cref{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential}, bound $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_I$, $(1+\\bar\\chi_t)/(1-\\bar\\chi_t)\\le (1+x_T)/(1-x_T)$, and $\\bar\\beta_t^{\\rm corr}\\le\\mathcal B_{T,r}^{\\rm corr}$, then use $\\gamma_T\\le\\mathcal G_{T,r}\\le\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{T,r}$. Finally substitute $2\\bar E_T\\le4c_\\mu R^2T/\\sqrt W$. The stated orders follow directly.',
    'The same display holds with $\\Gamma^{\\rm tail}_{T,r}$ replaced by the smaller $\\Gamma^{\\rm split}_{T,r}$ from~\\eqref{eq:gamma-split-main}. This is a realized-complexity statement: the policy may retain the existing observable $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}$ schedule, but a terminal realized $\\Delta_{T,r}$ is not used in any action-time radius. Because the deterministic spectral inequality holds for every $r$, the terminal report may minimize $\\Gamma^{\\rm tail}_{T,r}$ over $r$ a posteriori, provided that choice does not alter any past action, radius, or other action-time quantity.',
    'The sharpened sandwich in \\Cref{cor:drift-sandwich} and Loewner inversion give \\[ s_t(a)\\le\\frac{\\bar s_t(a)}{1-\\bar\\chi_t}. \\] The per-round proof of \\Cref{thm:regret}, together with $\\sqrt{u_t}=1+\\bar\\chi_t$, therefore gives \\[ \\operatorname{regret}_t\\le 2\\alpha_t\\omega_t \\frac{1+\\bar\\chi_t}{1-\\bar\\chi_t}\\bar s_t(a_t) +2\\epslin(t). \\] Sum and apply Cauchy--Schwarz once. The frozen rank-one update and the usual elliptic-potential inequality give $\\sum_t\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\le (\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\gamma_T$, proving the display. The corrected-center claim follows from the prediction identity in \\Cref{cor:corrected-center}.',
    'The stated exponential relative-drift certificate obeys $\\rho_W<1$ exactly when',
    'The transfer factor gives $\\bar s_s^2(a_s)\\le u_s(a_s)\\,s_{\\Calg,s}^2(a_s)$, and \\Cref{lem:cg} with $\\varepsilon_{s,a_s}\\le\\bar\\varepsilon_s$ gives $s_{\\Calg,s}^2(a_s)\\le\\tilde s_s^2(a_s)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_s)$; hence $\\bar s_s^2(a_s)\\le u_s(a_s)\\,\\tilde s_s^2(a_s)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_s)$. By monotonicity of $x\\mapsto\\log(1+\\sigma^{-2}x)$, each summand of $\\gamma_{t-1}$ in~\\eqref{eq:infogain} is dominated by the corresponding summand of $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}$. Predictability at round $t$ holds because every $\\tilde s_s^2(a_s)$, $u_s(a_s)$, and $\\bar\\varepsilon_s$ in the sum was fixed before the corresponding past reward and belongs to the prefix history $\\cHist_t^-$. The schedule property then follows term by term as in \\Cref{cor:beta-constructive}.',
    "The trust region gives $Q_t\\le4R^2(t-1)$. Substitution in \\eqref{eq:relative-link-psi} proves $\\bar\\psi_t^{\\rm rel}\\le\\Psi^{\\rm rel}_{T,W}$. Taylor's theorem gives $\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}\\le2c_hR^2/\\sqrt W$, and hence the stated bounds on $\\bar F_{T+1},\\bar E_T$ and the confidence radius \\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-relative-radius}. Apply \\Cref{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential}, bound the time-varying factors by $\\alpha_I$, $\\rho_W$, and $\\mathcal B^{\\rm rel}_{T,r}$, and use $\\gamma_T\\le\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{T,r}$ to obtain \\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-relative-regret}.",
    'The trust region gives $Q_t\\le4R^2(t-1)$; substitution in \\Cref{lem:path-certificates} proves all displayed path bounds. \\Cref{cor:drift-sandwich} gives the two-sided operator relation. The CG sandwich and $u_t\\le(1+x_T)^2=\\rho_+$ imply that every logarithm in $\\hat\\gamma_T$ is at most $\\log(1+A_{\\rm inf}\\sigma^{-2}\\bar s_t^2(a_t))$. For $A\\ge1$, $\\log(1+Ax)\\le A\\log(1+x)$, proving the information comparison. Substitution into \\Cref{cor:twosided} gives the regret display.',
    'This appendix expands the locked full aggregate summarized in \\Cref{sec:transport-instantiation}. The experiment protocol, configuration, selection record, aggregate, and every generated artifact carry provenance and         hashes. The figures use only the 50 evaluation seeds. Smoke and tuning results are absent.',
    'This is a \\emph{direct} argument through the monotone frozen Gram $\\bCbar_t$; it does \\emph{not} substitute $\\hat s_t$ for the dynamic width in \\Cref{cor:worstcase}. \\emph{Step 1 (variance sandwich).} Inverting the two-sided sandwich gives $\\rho_{+,t}^{-1}\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\preceq\\hat\\bC_t^{-1}\\preceq\\rho_{-,t}^{-1}\\bCbar_t^{-1}$, so, evaluating the quadratic form at $\\bg_t(a)$, $\\rho_{+,t}^{-1/2}\\,\\bar s_t(a)\\le\\hat s_t(a)\\le\\rho_{-,t}^{-1/2}\\,\\bar s_t(a)$. The lower half gives $\\bar s_t^2(a)\\le\\rho_{+,t}\\hat s_t^2(a)\\le u_t\\hat s_t^2(a)$, so \\Cref{asm:transfer} holds with $u_t=\\max\\{1,\\rho_{+,t}\\}$. \\emph{Step 2 (per-round regret).} With $s_{\\Calg,t}=\\hat s_t$, \\Cref{thm:regret} Steps~1--4 give $\\mathrm{regret}_t\\le 2\\,\\alpha_t\\,\\omega_t\\,\\sqrt{u_t}\\,\\hat s_t(a_t)+2\\epslin(t)$; using $\\hat s_t(a_t)\\le\\rho_{-,t}^{-1/2}\\bar s_t(a_t)$, $u_t=\\max\\{1,\\rho_{+,t}\\}\\le\\rho_+^{\\star}$, $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_I$, and $\\omega_t\\le\\omega_{\\max,T}$, \\[ \\mathrm{regret}_t \\;\\le\\; 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\omega_{\\max,T}\\,\\sqrt{\\rho_+^{\\star}/\\rho_{-,t}}\\;\\bar s_t(a_t) +2\\epslin(t) \\;\\le\\; 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\omega_{\\max,T}\\,\\sqrt{\\rho_+^{\\star}/\\rho_-}\\;\\bar s_t(a_t) +2\\epslin(t). \\] \\emph{Step 3 (frozen-feature elliptic potential).} Sum over $t$ and apply Cauchy--Schwarz, then the classical elliptic-potential identity for the \\emph{monotone} Gram $\\bCbar_t$ (\\eqref{eq:bar-update}), $\\sum_{t}\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\le(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\sum_t\\log(1+\\sigma^{-2} \\bar s_t^2(a_t))=(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,\\gamma_T$: \\[ R_T\\le 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\omega_{\\max,T}\\,\\sqrt{\\rho_+^{\\star}/\\rho_-} \\sqrt{T\\sum_t\\bar s_t^2(a_t)}+2E_T \\le 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\omega_{\\max,T}\\,\\sqrt{\\rho_+^{\\star}/\\rho_-} \\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,T\\,\\gamma_T}+2E_T. \\] The probability statement is the union bound over $\\cE_{\\mathrm{conf}}$ and $\\cE_{\\mathrm{cert}}$.',
    'This is a scaled generalized-linear family in the displacement coordinates, not a claim that generic neural-network width provides the scale $W$. Let \\[ c_h:=\\frac{4B_\\phi^2}{3\\sqrt3},\\qquad \\rho_W:=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{4B_\\phi R}{\\sqrt W}\\right)-1. \\] Then valid global constants on the certified region are',
    'This is a standard linear ridge regression with design vectors $\\bg_s/\\sigma$ that are $\\cG_s$-measurable before the corresponding noise, and Gram matrix~$\\bCbar_t$. The interleaved filtration $\\widetilde{\\cF}_{2s-1}=\\cG_s$, $\\widetilde{\\cF}_{2s}=\\cF_s$ supplies the pre-increment measurability required by the self-normalized martingale bound \\citep{abbasi2011improved}, which applies directly (\\Cref{lem:confidence}).',
    'This is the \\emph{only} spectral relation the one-sided fallback theorem needs, and it is one-sided: it dominates the predictable frozen-feature width $\\bar s_t(a)$ (the object of the self-normalized confidence bound) by an inflated algorithmic width. No \\emph{lower} Loewner factor on $\\Calg_t$ is required for optimism. The factor may be action-dependent; when a uniform per-round $u_t$ satisfies~\\eqref{eq:transfer} for every action we write $u_t$ and state the action-dependent theorem with $u_t(a)$. For the exact-curvature instantiation $\\Calg_t=\\bC_t$, the whitened relative-drift lemma below certifies~\\eqref{eq:transfer} with the uniform factor $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$; for an approximate operator, the composition of \\Cref{lem:whitened} with a one-sided spectral bound $\\hat\\bC_t\\preceq\\kappa_{+,t}\\bC_t$ gives $u_t=\\kappa_{+,t}(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ (\\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion}).',
    'This proves \\eqref{eq:sharp-transport-regret-new}. Finally, $\\alpha_te^{\\bar D_t}\\sqrt{\\kappa_{+,t}/\\kappa_{-,t}}\\ge1$, hence $1+x\\le2x$ gives \\eqref{eq:simple-transport-regret-new}. The probability statement follows by a union bound; independence is not required.',
    'This remains valid when $\\bar D_t\\ge1$. At $t=1$, the factor has zero rows, $Q_1=0$, and both metrics equal $\\lambda\\bI$.',
    'Two cases at value one must be distinguished. The true condition number $\\kappa_t=1$ forces all eigenvalues equal, i.e.\\ $\\Calg_t=c_t\\bI$ for some scalar $c_t>0$, and one CG iteration from $\\tilde\\bu_0=\\mathbf0$ is \\emph{exact} ($\\varepsilon_{t,a}=0$) for every $\\bg\\ne\\mathbf0$; this does \\emph{not} by itself imply $c_t=\\lambda$. With the specific certificate used here, $\\bar\\kappa_t=1$ additionally forces $\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Calg_t)=\\lambda$, which together with $\\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$ gives $\\Calg_t=\\lambda\\bI$ exactly. In either reading the single-step solve is exact, so we keep $\\bar\\kappa_t=1$ as a separate branch and never evaluate the logarithmic budget below at its zero denominator. For $\\bar\\kappa_t>1$, setting the right-hand side of the rate equal to $\\epsCGbar$ and requiring $\\epsCGbar < 1$ (where $\\epsCGbar$ denotes the round-$t$ target tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t$) gives the per-solve budget',
    'Under realizability, \\Cref{lem:bounded-output-residual} supplies the premise with probability at least $1-\\delta_R$ by taking',
    'Under the GGN/Fisher-linearized Gaussian approximation with covariance $\\Calg_t^{-1}$, the quantity $s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)$ is the metric-dependent predictive variance of the linearized reward at $(\\bx_t,a)$. The \\emph{exact} full-history instantiation of CC-UCB takes $\\Calg_t=\\bC_t$, the relinearized curvature~\\eqref{eq:curvature}; weighted replay, subsampling, sliding windows, periodic refreshes, and randomized sketches are special implementations of $\\Calg_t$ (\\S\\ref{sec:curvop}), covered by the theory only when the corresponding one-sided transfer factor is certified.',
    'Under the hypotheses of \\Cref{cor:rank-near-linear}, if the window policy uses the predictable rank envelope $\\Gamma_{t-1,r}$ instead of the realized $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}$ in its confidence radius, then $H_T\\le\\alpha_I(1+x_T)\\Omega^{\\rm win}_{T,r}$, where $\\Omega^{\\rm win}_{T,r}$ is the explicit $\\Omega_{T,r}$ in \\Cref{cor:rank-near-linear} with $A_{\\rm inf}=1$. Hence \\eqref{eq:growing-window-regret-main} contains no uncontrolled information-gain placeholder. The excitation premise is restrictive and standard in persistently excited identification; contextual diversity alone does not imply it. For $\\dim U=r>1$ one must choose $\\tau_w$ after a rank-$r$ burn-in, as the formal early charge records. The predictable rank schedule in \\Cref{cor:rank-near-linear} makes $H_T$ polylogarithmic under the same bounded-path conditions. These sufficient upper-bound exponents are not a Pareto frontier or a lower bound. When $U$ is supplied, the projected Gram in \\eqref{eq:projected-width} can instead be solved directly.',
    'Under~\\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-linear-width}, the exponential mean-value bound $e^x-1\\le(1+\\rho_0)x$ for $0\\le x\\le\\log(1+\\rho_0)$ gives $\\rho_W\\le4(1+\\rho_0)B_\\phi R/\\sqrt W$. Substitute this and $W\\ge CTP_T$ in~\\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-relative-psi} to prove \\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-psi-uniform}; the rate follows. Finally \\Cref{lem:bounded-output-residual} with $B_\\mu=B_\\phi R$ proves \\eqref{eq:scaled-tanh-residual-factor}.',
    'We next instantiate the reference-confidence interface. The corrected center keeps statistical estimation in the predictable collection-time features while using the current nonlinear prediction as its anchor. The resulting identity does not assume that the representation parameter minimizes a nonlinear loss. Let $\\sigma>0$ and let the reward obey $r_t=\\mu_t^*(a_t)+\\eta_t$, where, for every $u\\in\\R$,',
    'We recall the key objects: $\\bg_s(\\bx,a)=\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(\\bx,a)$ (frozen features), $\\bCbar_t=\\lambda\\bI+\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\bg_s(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bg_s(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top$ (frozen-feature Gram matrix), $\\bar s_t(a)=\\sqrt{\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a)}$, the algorithmic curvature $\\Calg_t$ with width $s_{\\Calg,t}(a)=\\sqrt{\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a)}$, the linearized ridge estimator $\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:thetahat-lin}), the combined width factor $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t$, the per-round CG inflation $\\alpha_t=\\sqrt{(1+\\bar\\varepsilon_t)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)}$, and the realized dynamic complexity $\\Lamalg_T$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:lam-alg}). Write $\\widehat\\mu_t(a)=\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t,a)$.',
    'We write $\\btheta_t$ for the parameter returned by the warm-started optimizer on~$\\cL_t$. Let $\\mathcal D_{t-1}:=\\{(\\bx_s,a_s,r_s):s<t\\}$ and $N_t:=|\\mathcal D_{t-1}|=t-1$. Equation~\\eqref{eq:erm} is a \\emph{full-history} objective. The curvature replay set $\\mathcal S_t\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,t-1\\}$ and its size $m_t:=|\\mathcal S_t|$ affect $\\Calg_t$, but do not replace $\\mathcal D_{t-1}$ in~\\eqref{eq:erm}. Thus a generic full-batch evaluation of $\\nabla\\cL_t$ replays $N_t$ observations even when $m_t\\ll N_t$. In convex settings (e.g.\\ a frozen backbone with a linear head), under exact optimization this coincides with the global minimizer; in nonconvex settings $\\btheta_t$ may be a local minimizer or approximate stationary point. The action-scaled centering certificate (\\Cref{asm:optim}) controls the action-projected discrepancy between $\\btheta_t$ and the auxiliary frozen-feature ridge estimator $\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$. It does not certify convergence to a global minimizer of the nonlinear objective.',
    'With $B=G^2/\\sigma^2$, the right-hand side of the sufficient condition~\\eqref{eq:subsample-n} is $O\\!\\bigl(L\\cdot[m^2G^4/(\\varepsilon^2\\lambda^2\\sigma^4) + mG^2/(\\varepsilon\\lambda\\sigma^2)]\\bigr)$. The first (variance) term dominates unless $\\varepsilon\\lambda\\sigma^2\\gtrsim mG^2$, so this worst-case sufficient threshold has a \\emph{quadratic leading dependence} on~$m=t{-}1$ (it is a sufficient upper bound on the required sample size, not an exact or necessary sample complexity). The condition~\\eqref{eq:subsample-n} is conservative and sufficient, not necessary, with worst-case leading dependence quadratic in $m$. If its threshold exceeds the population size $m=t{-}1$, it provides no nontrivial subsampling guarantee for $n<m$. Taking $n=m$ recovers the full population exactly for the separate reason that every summand is included.',
    'With exact realizability and a frozen linear model, $B_T=E_T^{\\rm cur}=0$. For fixed $d$ and problem constants, the leading term is then $O(e^D\\sqrt K\\,d\\sqrt T\\log T)$. Exact solves set $\\alpha=1$, exact current curvature sets $K=1$, and exact score maximization sets $\\xi_t=0$, recovering the standard LinUCB determinant-potential rate up to displayed constants.',
    'Writing $B_t:=(\\Calg_t^{+})^{-1/2}$, we have $\\bI+\\Xi_t=B_t\\Calg_t^{+}B_t+B_t(\\Calg_{t+1}-\\Calg_t^{+})B_t =B_t\\Calg_{t+1}B_t$, a congruence of the SPD matrix $\\Calg_{t+1}$ by the invertible symmetric $B_t$, hence SPD (in particular its eigenvalues exceed $-1$ with no smallness condition on the jump $\\Calg_{t+1}-\\Calg_t^{+}$), and',
    'Writing $\\mathbf c_t=(c_{1,t},\\ldots,c_{t-1,t})^\\top\\in\\R^{t-1}$, the $d\\times(t-1)$ matrices of \\Cref{lem:whitened} satisfy',
    'Writing $n_t=t-1$, also define the $(d+n_t)\\times d$ stacked matrices',
    'Writing $x_i=\\lambda_i(A)/\\lambda$, concavity of $\\log(1+x)$ after padding the spectrum with zeros gives the rank--trace term. Also \\[ \\log(1+x)=\\frac{(1+x)\\log(1+x)}x\\frac{x}{1+x}. \\] The first factor is increasing because its derivative is $(x-\\log(1+x))/x^2\\ge0$; bounding it at $q_A$ and summing proves the statistical-dimension term. For the frozen Gram, $\\operatorname{tr}(\\bCbar_{T+1}-\\lambda\\bI)\\le TG^2/\\sigma^2$, which gives \\eqref{eq:rank-gamma-main} (with $r_T=\\min\\{r,T\\}$).',
    '[$O(d)$-state path certificates]\\label{lem:path-certificates} Suppose $\\nabla_\\theta\\mu_\\theta(z)$ is $L_g$-Lipschitz and bounded by $G$ on a convex region containing the realized iterates. Before round $t$, let $n_t=t-1$, let $\\bar\\btheta_{<t}$ and $J_{<t}=\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\btheta_s-\\bar\\btheta_{<t}\\|^2$ summarize past iterates, and let $R_t^{\\rm col}=\\sum_{s<t}c_s^2$ with $c_s=\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(z_s)-r_s$. At $t=1$ use the initialization $\\bar\\btheta_{<1}=\\mathbf0$, $J_{<1}=Q_1=R_1^{\\rm col}=0$. Then, for all $t$, \\[ Q_t:=J_{<t}+n_t\\|\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta_{<t}\\|^2 =\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|^2 \\] and the following $\\cHist_t$-measurable quantities are valid:',
    '[1] \\REQUIRE Reward predictor $\\mu_{\\btheta}$, damping $\\lambda>0$, noise proxy $\\sigma$, confidence level $\\delta$, radius $S$, analytic constants $G,L_g,L_\\mu$; algorithmic curvature mode $\\Calg_t$ (exact full-history $\\bC_t$ or approximate $\\hat\\bC_t$) with certified one-sided factor $\\kappa_{+,t}$ and condition upper bound $\\bar\\kappa_t$; per-round CG tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t<1$ \\STATE Initialize $\\btheta_1$, certified $\\zeta_1\\ge\\|\\nabla\\cL_1(\\btheta_1)\\|$, $n\\gets0$, $\\bar\\btheta\\gets\\mathbf0$, $J\\gets0$, $R^{\\rm col}\\gets0$, $\\bar F\\gets0$, $\\hat\\gamma\\gets0$, and $\\mathcal D_0\\gets\\emptyset$ \\FOR{$t = 1, 2, \\ldots, T$} \\STATE Observe $\\bx_t$, $K_t$, and candidates $a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,K_t}$; set $\\cA_t=\\{a_{t,k}:k\\le K_t\\}$; draw pre-action randomness $\\Omega_t$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{$\\cHist_t$ of~\\eqref{eq:filtration}} \\STATE $Q_t\\gets J+n\\|\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta\\|^2$; $\\bar\\chi_t\\gets L_g\\sqrt{Q_t}/(\\sigma\\sqrt\\lambda)$; $u_t\\gets\\kappa_{+,t}(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ \\STATE $\\bar M_t\\gets\\sigma^{-2}[L_g\\sqrt{R^{\\rm col}Q_t} +(3GL_g/2)Q_t+(L_g^2/2)Q_t^{3/2}]$; $\\bar\\psi_t\\gets(\\zeta_t+\\bar M_t)/\\sqrt\\lambda$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{in a radius-$R$ region use $L_g^2RQ_t$ for the last term} \\STATE $\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin}\\gets(L_\\mu/2)(S+\\|\\btheta_t\\|)^2$; $\\bar\\beta_t\\gets\\sqrt{\\hat\\gamma+2\\log(1/\\delta)} +\\sqrt\\lambda S+\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{\\bar F}$; $\\omega_t\\gets\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{corrected center uses $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t$} \\STATE Form or receive a fixed $\\cHist_t$-measurable SPD matrix--vector oracle $\\bv\\mapsto\\Calg_t\\bv$, $\\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$, held fixed across all CG solves this round \\label{line:oracle-detailed} \\hfill\\COMMENT{exact/buffered special case $\\Calg_t\\bv=\\lambda\\bv +\\sigma^{-2}\\!\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}\\!w_s\\, \\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)[\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top\\bv]$, weights $w_s$ per \\S\\ref{sec:curvop}} \\FOR{$k=1,\\ldots,K_t$} \\STATE $a\\gets a_{t,k}$ \\STATE $\\widehat\\mu_t(a) \\gets \\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t, a)$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{mean prediction (or corrected center~\\eqref{eq:corrected-center})} \\STATE $\\bg_t(a) \\gets \\nabla_{\\btheta}\\,\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t, a)$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{gradient feature} \\IF{$\\bg_t(a)=\\mathbf 0$} \\STATE $\\tilde{s}_t^2(a) \\gets 0$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{skip CG; no residual ratio} \\ELSE \\STATE $\\tilde\\bu \\gets \\mathrm{CG}(\\Calg_t,\\;\\bg_t(a))$; stop only once $\\|r_I\\|/\\|\\bg_t(a)\\| \\le \\bar\\varepsilon_t/\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}$, or use a certified budget $I_t$ from \\eqref{eq:cg-budget} \\hfill\\COMMENT{recompute the original residual in floating point; a point check is not an enclosure} \\label{line:cg-detailed} \\STATE $\\tilde{s}_t^2(a) \\gets \\bg_t(a)^\\top \\tilde\\bu$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{approx.\\ predictive variance} \\ENDIF \\ENDFOR \\STATE $k_t\\gets\\min\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{1\\le k\\le K_t} \\Bigl[\\,\\widehat\\mu_t(a_{t,k}) + \\omega_t\\,\\sqrt{\\dfrac{u_t(a_{t,k})}{1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t}}\\, \\sqrt{\\tilde{s}_t^2(a_{t,k})}\\,\\Bigr]$; $a_t\\gets a_{t,k_t}$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{measurable smallest-index tie rule} \\label{line:ucb-detailed} \\STATE Play $a_t$, observe $r_t$ \\STATE $\\mathcal{D}_t \\gets \\mathcal{D}_{t-1} \\cup \\{(\\bx_t, a_t, r_t)\\}$ \\STATE $c_t\\gets\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t,a_t)-r_t$; $R^{\\rm col}\\gets R^{\\rm col}+c_t^2$; $\\bar F\\gets\\bar F+(\\bar\\epsilon_t^{\\rm lin})^2$; $\\hat\\gamma\\gets\\hat\\gamma+\\log[1+\\sigma^{-2} u_t\\tilde s_t^2(a_t)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)]$ \\STATE $n\\gets n+1$; $d\\gets\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta$; $\\bar\\btheta\\gets\\bar\\btheta+d/n$; $J\\gets J+d^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta)$ \\hfill\\COMMENT{vector Welford update} \\STATE Run $J^{\\rm opt}_{t+1}$ warm-started optimizer steps on the \\emph{full} data set $\\mathcal D_t$ to obtain $\\btheta_{t+1}$, projecting to the certified region if required \\STATE Certify $\\zeta_{t+1}\\ge\\|\\nabla\\cL_{t+1}(\\btheta_{t+1})\\|$; unless the terminal optimizer evaluation supplies this quantity, this requires one additional full-data gradient evaluation \\ENDFOR',
    '[1] \\STATE Observe $\\bx_t$, $K_t$, and candidates $a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,K_t}$; set $\\cA_t=\\{a_{t,k}:k\\le K_t\\}$. \\STATE Construct a fixed pre-reward SPD oracle $\\bv\\mapsto\\Calg_t\\bv$ with $\\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$. \\FOR{$k=1,\\ldots,K_t$} \\STATE Set $a=a_{t,k}$. \\STATE Compute $\\widehat\\mu_t(a)=\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t,a)$ and $\\bg_t(a)=\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_t,a)$. \\STATE If $\\bg_t(a)\\ne\\mathbf0$, solve $\\Calg_t\\bu=\\bg_t(a)$ separately by CG until the certified tolerance holds; otherwise set $\\tilde\\bu=\\mathbf0$. \\label{line:cg} \\STATE Set $\\tilde s_t^2(a)=\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\tilde\\bu$. \\ENDFOR \\STATE Set $k_t$ to the smallest index maximizing $\\widehat\\mu_t(a_{t,k})+\\omega_t\\sqrt{u_t(a_{t,k})/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)} \\sqrt{\\tilde s_t^2(a_{t,k})}$; play $a_t=a_{t,k_t}$. \\label{line:ucb} \\STATE Observe $r_t$, append $(\\bx_t,a_t,r_t)$, and update $\\btheta_{t+1}$.',
    '[1] \\STATE Observe the context and graph-measurable action correspondence $\\cA_t$; this defines $\\cHist_t^-$. \\STATE Take the prefix-determined $\\btheta_t$, $\\bVbar_t$, and $\\bV_t$; using the observed context, form $\\bq_t(a)$, $m_t^{\\rm corr}(a)$, and $b_t(a)$. \\STATE Choose $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable failure budgets before their corresponding draws; reveal the pre-reward randomness $\\Omega_t$. \\STATE Construct $\\Calg_t$ and obtain pre-reward certificates $\\bar D_t$, $\\kappa_{-,t}$, and $\\kappa_{+,t}$. \\STATE Obtain one jointly measurable solver upper-width map $\\hat s_t(a)$ over the score domain. \\STATE Return a $\\cHist_t$-measurable $\\xi_t$-approximate selector for the score in \\eqref{eq:transported-score-new}. \\STATE Observe the reward; update the frozen ridge estimator and \\eqref{eq:reference-update-new}.',
    '[Approximate-rank exact-current GGN] \\label{cor:approx-rank-current-ggn} Under \\Cref{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential}, on the same confidence event the following holds simultaneously for every $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}$:',
    '[Bonus sandwiching]\\label{lem:bonus} Under \\Cref{asm:transfer} and $\\max_{a\\in\\cA_t}\\varepsilon_{t,a}\\le\\bar\\varepsilon_t<1$, the bonus~\\eqref{eq:bonus} satisfies, for every $a\\in\\cA_t$,',
    '[Bounded gradient features]\\label{asm:bounded} For a constant $G>0$, \\[ \\sup_{t\\le T}\\;\\sup_{s\\le t}\\;\\sup_{a} \\|\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_s,a)\\| \\;\\le\\; G. \\] This bounds both the current-round features $\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a)$ and the replayed features $\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)$ appearing in the curvature matrix~\\eqref{eq:curvature}, as well as the frozen features $\\bg_s(\\bx_s,a_s)$ in~\\eqref{eq:curvature-bar}.',
    '[Bounded-output residual energy] \\label{lem:bounded-output-residual} Fix a horizon $T\\ge1$ and $\\delta_R\\in(0,1)$. Suppose $|\\mu_{\\btheta}(x,a)|\\le B_\\mu$ throughout the certified parameter region and $|\\mu^*(x,a)|\\le B_\\mu$. Under \\Cref{asm:noise}, with probability at least $1-\\delta_R$, simultaneously for every $t\\le T+1$,',
    '[Bounded-path near-linear rate]\\label{cor:near-linear} Fix $T$ and suppose for every $t\\le T$ \\[ \\|\\btheta_t\\|,\\|\\btheta^*\\|\\le R,\\quad L_\\mu\\le c_\\mu/\\sqrt W,\\quad L_g\\le c_g/\\sqrt W,\\quad \\zeta_t\\le\\zeta_0/\\sqrt W,\\quad R_t^{\\rm col}\\le C_RtP_T , \\] where $P_T\\ge1$ is an explicitly supplied residual-energy factor. For exact current-parameter curvature and a uniform CG tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\le\\bar\\varepsilon<1$, set \\[ x_T=\\frac{2c_gR\\sqrt{T-1}}{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\lambda W}},\\quad \\rho_-=(1-x_T)^2,\\qquad \\rho_+=(1+x_T)^2. \\] If $x_T<1$, then $\\rho_-\\bCbar_t\\preceq\\bC_t\\preceq\\rho_+\\bCbar_t$ for all $t\\le T$. Writing \\[ \\alpha_I=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\bar\\varepsilon}{1-\\bar\\varepsilon}},\\qquad A_{\\rm inf}=\\alpha_I^2\\frac{\\rho_+}{\\rho_-}, \\] the observable information schedule satisfies $\\gamma_T\\le\\hat\\gamma_T\\le A_{\\rm inf}\\gamma_T$, and',
    '[CG multiplicative accuracy]\\label{lem:cg} Fix a round~$t$ and an action~$a$ with right-hand side $\\bg:=\\bg_t(a)\\ne\\mathbf0$. Let $\\bu = \\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg$ and let $\\tilde\\bu$ be the iterate after $I_{t,a}$ steps of CG on the SPD system $\\Calg_t\\bu=\\bg$ starting from $\\tilde\\bu_0=\\mathbf{0}$, with $\\Calg_t$ held \\emph{fixed} across all iterations. The relative energy-norm error is the per-action quantity $\\varepsilon_{t,a}$ of~\\eqref{eq:cg-err-eta}, and',
    '[Computability and dimension dependence of $\\beta_t$] Two caveats about $\\beta_t$ deserve emphasis. First, $\\gamma_{t-1}$ is defined through the frozen-feature Gram matrix $\\bCbar_t$, whose per-sample Jacobians the matrix-free implementation deliberately does not store; $\\gamma_{t-1}$ is therefore not directly computable in the stated memory model. Second, the worst-case substitute $\\gamma_{t-1}\\le d\\log\\bigl(1+(t-1)G^2/(d\\lambda\\sigma^2)\\bigr)$ does not exploit low realized information gain and can be order $t$ when $d\\gg t$, rendering the resulting bonus and theorem bound vacuous. \\Cref{cor:gammahat} addresses both issues with an observable surrogate built from quantities the algorithm already computes; the resulting guarantee is data-dependent, and its tightness still depends on the realized surrogate together with the other theorem factors.',
    '[Conditional growing-window bound under fixed-subspace excitation]\\label{thm:growing-window} Adopt the hypotheses, events, and valid predictable schedules of \\Cref{thm:regret}, let $m_t=\\min\\{t-1,\\lceil t^q\\rceil\\}$ for $q\\in(0,1]$, and let $\\Calg_t$ be the unrescaled current-parameter Gram over the last $m_t$ observations. Thus \\Cref{prop:window} gives $\\kappa_{+,t}=1$ and $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$. Suppose candidate gradients lie in a fixed subspace $U$, and let $P_U$ be its orthogonal projector. For a supplied burn-in index $\\tau_w\\in\\{1,\\ldots,T+1\\}$, assume the predictable window-excitation condition \\[ \\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI+\\kappa_wm_tP_U \\] holds for a known $\\kappa_w>0$ and every $t\\ge\\tau_w$. Then, on the same event of probability at least $1-\\delta-\\delta_{\\rm cert}$ as \\Cref{thm:regret},',
    '[Conditional projected-Gram rate for a supplied subspace]\\label{cor:rank-near-linear} Under \\Cref{cor:near-linear}, additionally suppose a subspace $U\\subseteq\\R^d$ with $\\dim U\\le r$ is fixed and supplied before interaction, and every candidate or replayed tangent feature through round $T$ lies in $U$. Hence $\\operatorname{rank}(\\bCbar_t-\\lambda\\bI)\\le r$ for every $t\\le T+1$. Let $r_U=\\dim U$, let $Q_U\\in\\R^{d\\times r_U}$ have orthonormal columns spanning $U$, and define \\[ \\mathbf z_{t,s}=Q_U^\\top\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s),\\qquad \\mathbf z_t(a)=Q_U^\\top\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a). \\] Then the current-GGN width reduces exactly to',
    '[Confidence for the linearized ridge estimator] \\label{lem:confidence} Suppose \\Cref{asm:noise,asm:bounded,asm:linear} hold and $\\mu^*=\\mu_{\\btheta^*}$ with $\\|\\btheta^*\\|\\le S$. Define the linearized ridge estimator~$\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$ by~\\eqref{eq:thetahat-lin}, the frozen-feature variance $\\bar s_t(a)=\\sqrt{\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a)}$, and the cumulative squared linearization error $F_t := \\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\\epslin(s)^2$. Then with probability at least $1-\\delta$, for all $t\\ge 1$ and all $a\\in\\cA_t$ simultaneously,',
    '[Corrected-center confidence without optimizer assumptions] \\label{cor:corrected-center-new} With probability at least $1-\\delta$, simultaneously over rounds and actions,',
    '[Corrected-center near-linear rate] \\label{cor:corrected-near-linear} Fix $T$ and suppose, for every $t\\le T$, \\[ \\|\\btheta_t\\|,\\|\\btheta^*\\|\\le R,\\qquad L_\\mu\\le c_\\mu/\\sqrt W,\\qquad L_g\\le c_g/\\sqrt W, \\] where $W$ is an abstract near-linearity scale. Use the corrected center, exact current curvature, and a uniform CG tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\le\\bar\\varepsilon<1$. Fix $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}$ and suppose a predictable prefix information envelope $\\mathcal G_{n,r}\\ge\\gamma_n$ is supplied for $n=0,\\ldots,T$. Valid choices are the rank envelope $\\Gamma_{n,r}$ under a known rank bound, or the spectral-tail envelope in \\Cref{cor:approx-rank-current-ggn} with supplied prefix tails $\\bar\\Delta_{n,r}$. Define its predictable monotone closure $\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{n,r}:=\\max_{0\\le j\\le n}\\mathcal G_{j,r}$ and set',
    '[Cost of the corrected center] Exact evaluation requires a solve against $\\bCbar_t$. A constant number of $O(d)$ CG vectors is insufficient by itself: one additionally needs stored historical frozen gradients, raw replay data with the historical parameter checkpoints needed to reconstruct them, a frozen-feature matrix--vector oracle, an explicit low-dimensional representation, or a certified sketch. Maintaining $\\bCbar_t$ explicitly costs $O(d^2)$ memory. Approximate frozen-feature access introduces an error that must be returned to the centering certificate unless separately controlled.',
    '[Dynamic log-determinant potential]\\label{lem:dynamic-potential} Let $\\{\\Calg_t\\}_{t\\ge1}$ satisfy~\\eqref{eq:calg-props} with $\\Calg_1=\\lambda\\bI$. For the played feature $\\bh_t:=\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)$ define the rank-one update $\\Calg_t^{+}:=\\Calg_t+\\sigma^{-2}\\bh_t\\bh_t^\\top$ and, using the symmetric (principal) root $(\\Calg_t^{+})^{-1/2}$, the normalized perturbation',
    '[Dynamic transfer regret bound for CC-UCB (Gaussian/squared-loss)]\\label{thm:regret} Under the conditions above and \\Cref{asm:noise,asm:bounded,asm:linear,asm:optim,asm:transfer}, suppose the measurably enumerated finite-domain protocol above holds and \\Cref{alg:ccucb} enumerates every active index using its smallest-index score rule, a predictable schedule $\\bar\\beta_t\\ge\\beta_t$, and uniform per-round CG tolerance. The finite score vector is pre-reward measurable; its maximum and smallest maximizing index are measurable. Hence the selected action, realized design, and regret are well-defined random variables. Let $E_T=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\epslin(t)$, $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t$, $\\alpha_t=\\sqrt{(1+\\bar\\varepsilon_t)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)}$, and $S_T=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\alpha_t^2u_t(a_t)\\omega_t^2$. On $\\cE_{\\mathrm{conf}}\\cap\\cE_{\\mathrm{cert}}$,',
    '[Estimator- and solver-agnostic confidence transport] \\label{thm:transported-ucb} Under the protocol above with $\\lambda>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, suppose \\Cref{asm:abstract-transport} holds, the score obeys \\eqref{eq:transported-score-new}--\\eqref{eq:approx-score-oracle-new}, and $\\|\\bq_t(a)\\|_2\\le G$. On the joint confidence and certificate event, the instantaneous regret satisfies',
    '[Exact-current regret through the frozen potential] \\label{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential} Adopt \\Cref{thm:regret} with exact current curvature $\\Calg_t=\\bC_t$. Suppose a predictable certificate satisfies $\\chi_t\\le\\bar\\chi_t<1$ for every $t$, and use the valid transfer factor $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$. Then, on the event of \\Cref{thm:regret},',
    '[GGN approximation error]\\label{lem:ggn-approx} Suppose $\\mu_{\\btheta}(\\bx,a)$ is twice differentiable in~$\\btheta$. Suppose $|r_s - \\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_s,a_s)| \\le \\epsilon_r$ and $\\|\\nabla^2 \\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_s,a_s)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}} \\le H$ for all $s < t$. Define $\\zeta := (t{-}1)\\,\\epsilon_r\\, H / (\\sigma^2 \\lambda)$. Then:',
    '[Gap-dependent exact-current regret] \\label{cor:gap-dependent-exact-current} Adopt \\Cref{cor:exact-current-frozen-potential}. Define the realized gap $\\Delta_t:=\\mu^*(\\bx_t,a_t^*)-\\mu^*(\\bx_t,a_t)$ and suppose every suboptimal candidate action at every round has gap at least a deterministic (or almost-sure) $\\Delta_{\\min}>0$. This gap is an analysis condition and is not used to tune the policy; if it holds only on a high-probability event, that event must be added to the certificate failure allocation. Suppose also $\\epslin(t)\\le\\Delta_{\\min}/4$ for all $t$, and set \\[ H_t^{\\rm gap}:=\\alpha_t\\omega_t \\frac{1+\\bar\\chi_t}{1-\\bar\\chi_t},\\qquad H_{\\max}^{\\rm gap}:=\\max_{t\\le T}H_t^{\\rm gap}. \\] Then, on the event of \\Cref{thm:regret},',
    '[Global near-linearity under exponential transport] \\label{cor:global-near-linear-new} Assume exact realizability, $\\mu^*(z)=\\mu_{\\btheta^\\circ}(z)$, and suppose $\\|\\btheta^\\circ\\|_2\\le R$ and $\\|\\btheta_t\\|_2\\le R$ for every $t$. On the certified convex radius-$R$ region, assume the prediction gradient is $L_\\mu$-Lipschitz for the Taylor envelopes and satisfies the replay-path Hessian bound $L_g$ in \\Cref{cor:hessian-path-new}, with \\[ L_\\mu\\le\\frac{c_\\mu}{\\sqrt W},\\qquad L_g\\le\\frac{c_g}{\\sqrt W}, \\] with $W>0$. Use the corrected center, exact current curvature, exact dense solves, and exact score maximization in finite parameter dimension $d$, and suppose $\\|\\bq_t(a)\\|_2\\le G$. Thus $\\alpha_t=1$, $\\kappa_{-,t}=\\kappa_{+,t}=1$, and $\\xi_t=0$. Define \\[ D_{T,W}:=\\frac{4c_gR\\sqrt{T-1}}{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\lambda W}}. \\] Then, with probability at least $1-\\delta$,',
    '[Hessian path certificate] \\label{cor:hessian-path-new} Suppose the parameter region is convex and $\\|\\nabla_\\theta^2\\mu_\\theta(x_s,a_s)\\|_{\\rm op}\\le L_g$ along every segment in \\eqref{eq:scalar-factor-path-new}. With',
    '[Inverse-quadratic interval from the original residual] \\label{lem:inverse-quadratic-certificate-new} Let $\\Calg=\\Calg^\\top\\succeq\\lambda_{\\Calg}\\bI$ with $\\lambda_{\\Calg}>0$. For an approximate solution $\\tilde\\bu$ to $\\Calg\\bu=\\bq$, recompute the original residual $\\br=\\bq-\\Calg\\tilde\\bu$. In exact arithmetic,',
    '[Legacy one-sided sanity check: frozen features recover linear UCB]\\label{cor:frozen} Suppose the backbone is frozen and the reward head is linear in its parameters, the ridge objective is optimized exactly, and take the algorithmic curvature to be the sequential frozen Gram matrix, $\\Calg_t=\\bCbar_t$. Then: $\\epslin(t)=0$ for all~$t$ ($L=0$ in \\Cref{asm:linear}), so $E_T=F_T=0$; $\\bar\\psi_t=0$ (under exact optimization the MAP and linearized estimators coincide, so \\Cref{asm:optim} holds with equality), giving $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t$; $u_t(a)=1$ for all $a$ (indeed $\\bar s_t^2(a)=s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)$, so \\Cref{asm:transfer} holds with equality); and, since $\\bCbar_{t+1}=\\bCbar_t+\\sigma^{-2}\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)^\\top$ by~\\eqref{eq:bar-update}, the update is exactly the rank-one step, i.e.\\ $\\Calg_{t+1}=\\Calg_t^{+}$, so $\\Xi_t=0$, $\\Valg_T=0$, and $\\Lamalg_T=\\gamma_T$. Then \\Cref{thm:regret} gives \\[ R_T \\;\\le\\; 2\\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,\\gamma_T\\, \\sum_{t=1}^T \\alpha_t^2\\,\\bar\\beta_t^2}. \\] If in addition $\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\le\\bar\\varepsilon$ so that $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_I:=\\sqrt{(1+\\bar\\varepsilon)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon)}$, the looser \\Cref{cor:worstcase} recovers the familiar LinUCB form $R_T\\le 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\bar\\beta_{\\max,T}\\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,T\\,\\gamma_T}$, where $\\bar\\beta_{\\max,T}:=\\max_{t\\le T}\\bar\\beta_t$.',
    '[Linear-subclass lower bound] The exact-rank class contains a standard $r$-dimensional stochastic linear bandit. Take a constant context, a known orthonormal basis $Q_U$, the finite action set $z(a)\\in\\{\\pm1/\\sqrt r\\}^r$, linear mean $\\mu_\\vartheta(a)=z(a)^\\top\\vartheta$, and Gaussian noise. Choosing the usual hypercube parameter family with coordinates of order $\\sqrt{r/T}$ keeps the parameter norm bounded when $T\\gtrsim r^2$. Here $L_\\mu=L_g=E_T=0$, $\\bC_t=\\bCbar_t$, and all features lie in $U$. The stochastic linear-bandit minimax expected-regret lower bound is $\\Omega(r\\sqrt T)$ in this regime \\citep{dani2008stochastic}. Hence the leading $r\\sqrt T$ dependence of the exact-rank upper bound is sharp up to logarithms on this linear subclass. This does not prove a lower bound for the nonlinear or spectral-tail classes, nor a computational lower bound.',
    '[Logarithmic comparison along an SPD path] \\label{lem:log-spd-path} Let $\\bV:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb{S}_{++}^d$ be entrywise absolutely continuous and let $\\dot\\bV(\\tau)$ denote its almost-everywhere derivative. If',
    '[Looser worst-case reduction]\\label{cor:worstcase} If $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_{\\max}$, $u_t(a_t)\\le u_{\\max}$, and $\\omega_t\\le\\omega_{\\max}$ for all $t\\le T$, then $S_T\\le\\alpha_{\\max}^2 u_{\\max}\\omega_{\\max}^2T$ and \\[ R_T \\;\\le\\; 2\\alpha_{\\max}\\omega_{\\max}\\sqrt{u_{\\max}} \\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)T\\Lamalg_T}+2E_T . \\] This $\\sqrt{T\\Lamalg_T}$ form is weakly looser than \\eqref{eq:regret-bound}, with equality when the per-round factors are constant. It is recorded only as a legacy reduction, not as the main result.',
    '[Measurably enumerated finite domains] \\label{cor:finite-enumeration-new} Suppose there is a positive $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable integer $K_t$ and $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable actions $a_{t,k}:\\Omega\\to\\mathsf A$, $k\\ge1$, such that \\[ \\cA_t=\\{a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,K_t}\\}. \\] If the learner chooses the smallest index attaining the score maximum, then the selected action is $\\cHist_t$-measurable and \\eqref{eq:approx-score-oracle-new} holds with $\\xi_t=0$. The score and mean-reward suprema and the universal actionwise events reduce to measurable finite maxima and intersections. A fixed finite action set is the immediate special case.',
    '[Nonlinear tanh-link Gaussian bandit]\\label{cor:tanh-link} For $\\mu_\\theta(x,a)=\\tanh(\\phi(x,a)^\\top\\theta)$ with $\\|\\phi(x,a)\\|\\le B_\\phi$, one may take \\[ G=B_\\phi,\\qquad L_\\mu=L_g=\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt3}B_\\phi^2. \\] On any certified trust region, \\Cref{cor:operational} therefore gives an executable nonlinear Gaussian-reward CC-UCB policy using only the online path statistics, a pre-action optimizer-residual certificate, and the stated operator/CG certificates. Under realizability, both the model and teacher outputs have $B_\\mu=1$. Hence \\Cref{lem:bounded-output-residual} removes the supplied residual-energy factor in \\Cref{cor:near-linear}: on its event one may take \\[ C_R=1,\\qquad P_T=8+4\\sigma^2\\log(2T/\\delta_R), \\] and allocate $\\delta_R$ to the certificate failure budget.',
    '[Observable CG upper bound on the width complexity]\\label{cor:lam-observable} If the played-action CG solve satisfies $\\varepsilon_{t,a_t}\\le\\bar\\varepsilon_t<1$ so that $\\tilde s_t^2(a_t)\\ge(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)\\,s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a_t)$ (\\Cref{lem:cg}), then',
    '[Observable information-gain surrogate via the one-sided transfer factor]\\label{cor:gammahat} Run \\Cref{alg:ccucb} with a pre-reward algorithmic curvature sequence~$\\Calg_s$, per-round CG tolerance with $\\varepsilon_{s,a_s}\\le\\bar\\varepsilon_s<1$ for the played action, and an $\\cHist_s$-measurable one-sided optimism-transfer factor $u_s(a)\\ge1$ satisfying $\\bar s_s^2(a)\\le u_s(a)\\,s_{\\Calg,s}^2(a)$ (\\Cref{asm:transfer}). Suppose also that the nonnegative predictable sequence $\\bar F_t$ satisfies $\\bar F_t\\ge F_t$. Define the observable information-gain surrogate \\[ \\hat\\gamma_{t-1} \\;:=\\; \\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\\log\\!\\Bigl(1+\\sigma^{-2}\\, \\frac{u_s(a_s)}{1-\\bar\\varepsilon_s}\\, \\tilde s_s^2(a_s)\\Bigr), \\] where $\\tilde s_s^2(a_s)=\\bg_s(a_s)^\\top\\tilde\\bu_s$ is the CG variance proxy computed at round~$s$ for the played action. Then $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}$ is $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable and $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}\\ge\\gamma_{t-1}$, so \\[ \\hat\\beta_t \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}+2\\log(1/\\delta)} \\;+\\;\\sqrt{\\lambda}\\,S \\;+\\;\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{\\bar F_t} \\] is a predictable schedule with $\\hat\\beta_t\\ge\\beta_t$. Played-action CG accuracy is sufficient for this information comparison. If the same tolerance also holds uniformly for every candidate action before selection, then \\Cref{thm:regret} holds with $\\bar\\beta_t=\\hat\\beta_t$.',
    '[One-sided optimism transfer]\\label{asm:transfer} For every round $t\\le T$ there is an $\\cHist_t$-measurable factor $u_t(a)\\ge 1$ such that, for every candidate action $a\\in\\cA_t$,',
    '[Pointwise CG and PCG solver certificates] \\label{cor:cg-interface-new} Suppose CG or PCG supplies, in exact arithmetic, an original-system energy certificate $\\|\\bu-\\tilde\\bu\\|_{\\Calg}\\le\\varepsilon\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg}$ with $\\varepsilon<1$. Then \\[ (1-\\varepsilon)s_{\\Calg}^2 \\le\\bq^\\top\\tilde\\bu \\le(1+\\varepsilon)s_{\\Calg}^2. \\] Thus \\[ \\hat s=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\bq^\\top\\tilde\\bu}{1-\\varepsilon}}, \\qquad \\alpha=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\varepsilon}{1-\\varepsilon}} \\] give a pointwise upper-valid width and sharpness factor for this query. A jointly measurable action-indexed family of such solves, together with a simultaneous energy-certificate event for every scored action, supplies the all-domain upper condition in \\eqref{eq:solver-interface-new}; the sharpness conclusion is needed only at the played action. For a general standard-Borel domain, measurability of the family and simultaneous functional validity remain additional premises. A preconditioner may change the recurrence, but certificate checks must use the original operator.',
    '[Predictable confidence schedule]\\label{cor:beta-constructive} Suppose a nonnegative $\\cHist_t^-$-measurable sequence $\\{\\bar F_t\\}_{t\\ge 1}$ satisfies $F_t \\le \\bar F_t$ for all~$t$. Then \\Cref{alg:ccucb} may set \\[ \\bar\\beta_t \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\gamma_{t-1} + 2\\log(1/\\delta)} \\;+\\; \\sqrt{\\lambda}\\,S \\;+\\; \\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{\\bar F_t}, \\] which is predictable and satisfies $\\bar\\beta_t \\ge \\beta_t$.',
    '[Primitive upper bound for prediction discrepancy]\\label{lem:primitive-pred} Let $z_s=(\\bx_s,a_s)$, $\\bg_s=\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(z_s)$, $\\bg_{s,t}=\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(z_s)$, and $\\delta_{s,t}=\\bg_{s,t}-\\bg_s$. Define \\[ \\bar\\mu_{s,t} := \\mu_{\\btheta_s}(z_s)+\\bg_s^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s), \\qquad e_{s,t}:=\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(z_s)-\\bar\\mu_{s,t}, \\qquad \\bar r_{s,t}:=\\bar\\mu_{s,t}-r_s . \\] Let $\\bar{\\cL}_t$ be the frozen-feature linearized ridge objective whose minimizer is $\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$, and define \\[ M_t := \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2} \\sum_{s<t} \\left[ \\bar r_{s,t}\\delta_{s,t} + e_{s,t}\\bg_s + e_{s,t}\\delta_{s,t} \\right]. \\] If the nonlinear optimizer satisfies $\\|\\nabla \\cL_t(\\btheta_t)\\|_2\\le\\zeta_t$, then for every candidate action $a\\in\\cA_t$, \\[ \\left| \\bg_t(a)^\\top (\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}) \\right| \\;\\le\\; \\bar s_t(a) \\left( \\frac{\\zeta_t}{\\sqrt{\\lambda}} + \\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\right), \\qquad \\bar s_t(a):=\\sqrt{\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a)} . \\] The left-hand side is exactly the geometric bound $|\\bg_t(a)^\\top(\\btheta_t-\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}})|\\le\\bar s_t(a)\\,\\psi_t$ of \\Cref{asm:optim} with $\\psi_t=\\zeta_t/\\sqrt\\lambda+\\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}$ (\\Cref{eq:psi-def}). Consequently, since $\\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}\\le\\|M_t\\|_2/\\sqrt{\\lambda}$, a valid centering certificate is \\[ \\bar\\psi_t \\;:=\\; \\frac{\\zeta_t+\\|M_t\\|_2}{\\sqrt{\\lambda}} \\;\\ge\\; \\psi_t . \\] If, in addition, on a fixed event, $\\btheta\\mapsto\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta}(z)$ is $L_g$-Lipschitz, $|\\bar r_{s,t}|\\le B_{t,\\delta}$ for all $s<t$, and $\\Delta_{s,t}:=\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|$, then \\[ \\|M_t\\|_2 \\;\\le\\; \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2} \\sum_{s<t} \\left[ B_{t,\\delta} L_g\\Delta_{s,t} + \\frac{G L_g}{2}\\Delta_{s,t}^2 + \\frac{L_g^2}{2}\\Delta_{s,t}^3 \\right]. \\]',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{cor:finite-d-rate-new}] Let $A_T=\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{t=1}^T\\bq_t(a_t)\\bq_t(a_t)^\\top$. Its rank is at most $r_T=\\min\\{d,T\\}$ and its trace is at most $TG^2/\\sigma^2$. Concavity of $x\\mapsto\\log(1+x)$ over the nonzero eigenvalues gives \\[ \\gamma_T=\\log\\det(\\bI+A_T/\\lambda) \\le r_T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{TG^2}{r_T\\lambda\\sigma^2}\\right). \\] The radius in \\eqref{eq:beta-corrected-new} is bounded by the bracketed term in \\eqref{eq:finite-d-rate-new-display}. Substitute this bound, the uniform transport factors, and \\eqref{eq:finite-d-gamma-new} into \\eqref{eq:simple-transport-regret-new}. The current bias envelope is exactly $b_t(a_t)=\\epsilon_{\\rm lin,t}(a_t)+\\epsilon_{\\rm mis,t}(a_t)$.',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{cor:finite-enumeration-new}] On the measurable event $\\{K_t=n\\}$, the finite vector $\\{U_t(a_{t,k})\\}_{k=1}^n$ is $\\cHist_t$-measurable by joint measurability on the graph. Its maximum and the smallest maximizing index are measurable. A countable partition over $n\\ge1$ therefore gives a $\\cHist_t$-measurable random index and hence a measurable selected action. The score maximum is attained, so $\\xi_t=0$. The same finite-max argument applies to the mean-reward supremum, and each universal event is a finite intersection on $\\{K_t=n\\}$.',
    "[Proof of \\Cref{cor:global-near-linear-new}] The radius bound gives \\[ Q_t\\le\\sum_{s<t} (\\|\\btheta_t\\|_2+\\|\\btheta_s\\|_2)^2 \\le4R^2(t-1). \\] Hence \\Cref{cor:hessian-path-new} gives $\\bar D_t\\le D_{T,W}$. Taylor's theorem and $\\|\\btheta^\\circ-\\btheta_s\\|_2\\le2R$ give \\[ \\epsilon_{\\rm lin}(s),\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm lin,t}(a) \\le\\frac{2c_\\mu R^2}{\\sqrt W}. \\] Exact realizability removes every misspecification envelope, so \\[ B_T\\le\\frac{4c_\\mu^2R^4T}{W},\\qquad E_T^{\\rm cur}\\le\\frac{2c_\\mu R^2T}{\\sqrt W}. \\] Substitute these inequalities, $S=R$, $\\alpha=1$, $K=1$, and $\\xi_t=0$ into \\Cref{cor:finite-d-rate-new}. The asymptotic statement follows from $\\Gamma_{T,d}=O(d\\log T)$ for fixed problem constants.",
    '[Proof of \\Cref{cor:hessian-path-new}] The $s$th factor-row derivative is \\[ \\sigma^{-1}\\left[ \\nabla_\\theta^2\\mu_{\\btheta_s+\\tau(\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s)}(x_s,a_s) (\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s)\\right]^\\top. \\] Hence $\\|\\dot\\Bmat_t(\\tau)\\|_F^2\\le L_g^2Q_t/\\sigma^2$. Since $\\bV_t(\\tau)\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$, \\[ \\dot\\Bmat_t(\\tau)^\\top\\dot\\Bmat_t(\\tau) \\preceq\\frac{L_g^2Q_t}{\\sigma^2\\lambda}\\bV_t(\\tau). \\] Apply \\Cref{lem:factor-spd-path} with the constant $\\nu_t=L_g\\sqrt{Q_t}/(\\sigma\\sqrt\\lambda)$.',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{cor:local-small-drift-new}] Inverting \\eqref{eq:local-additive-new} gives \\[ \\bar s_t(a)\\le(1+\\chi_t)s_t(a),\\qquad s_t(a)\\le(1-\\chi_t)^{-1}\\bar s_t(a). \\] The first inequality, the upper operator comparison, and solver upper validity make the local score optimistic. At the played action, solver sharpness, the lower operator comparison, and the second inequality bound its width term by \\[ \\alpha_t\\frac{1+\\chi_t}{1-\\chi_t} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\kappa_{+,t}}{\\kappa_{-,t}}}\\,\\bar s_t(a_t). \\] Repeat the proof of \\Cref{thm:transported-ucb} and apply \\eqref{eq:frozen-potential-proof}.',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{lem:factor-spd-path}] Almost everywhere, $\\dot\\bV=\\dot\\Bmat^\\top\\Bmat+\\Bmat^\\top\\dot\\Bmat$. Put $E=\\dot\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2}$ and $H=\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2}$. Then \\[ \\bV^{-1/2}\\dot\\bV\\bV^{-1/2}=E^\\top H+H^\\top E. \\] Moreover, \\[ H^\\top H =\\bV^{-1/2}\\Bmat^\\top\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2} =\\bI-\\lambda\\bV^{-1}\\preceq\\bI, \\] so $\\|H\\|_{\\rm op}\\le1$. Therefore the normalized SPD speed is at most $2\\|E\\|_{\\rm op}$, which proves \\eqref{eq:factor-path-bound-new} after integration. For the PSD form, congruence by $\\bV^{-1/2}$ gives \\[ \\|\\dot\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}^2 =\\|\\bV^{-1/2}\\dot\\Bmat^\\top\\dot\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op} \\le\\nu_t(\\tau)^2. \\] Because $\\nu_t(\\tau)\\ge0$, taking square roots gives $\\|\\dot\\Bmat\\bV^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\nu_t(\\tau)$ almost everywhere. The assumed $L^1$ integrability permits integration in \\eqref{eq:factor-path-bound-new}.',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{lem:log-spd-path}] Fix $v\\ne0$ and set $f_v(\\tau)=v^\\top\\bV(\\tau)v$. The path is continuous and positive definite on a compact interval, so $f_v$ is absolutely continuous and bounded away from zero. Thus $\\log f_v$ is absolutely continuous and, almost everywhere, \\[ \\frac{d}{d\\tau}\\log f_v(\\tau) =\\frac{v^\\top\\dot\\bV(\\tau)v}{v^\\top\\bV(\\tau)v}. \\] With $w=\\bV(\\tau)^{1/2}v$, the absolute value of this derivative is at most $\\|\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\dot\\bV(\\tau) \\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}$. Integrating gives \\[ e^{-\\mathcal D(\\bV)}v^\\top\\bV(0)v \\le v^\\top\\bV(1)v \\le e^{\\mathcal D(\\bV)}v^\\top\\bV(0)v. \\] The inequality holds for every $v$, which is exactly \\eqref{eq:log-path-sandwich-new}. Inverting the sandwich gives the corresponding inverse-quadratic-form comparison. The proof never differentiates a matrix square root.',
    '[Proof of \\Cref{prop:width-transport-new}] The logarithmic sandwich and inversion give \\[ \\bar s_t^2(a)\\le e^{\\bar D_t}s_t^2(a),\\qquad s_t^2(a)\\le e^{\\bar D_t}\\bar s_t^2(a). \\] Inverting the operator sandwich gives \\[ s_t^2(a)\\le\\kappa_{+,t}s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a),\\qquad s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a)\\le\\kappa_{-,t}^{-1}s_t^2(a). \\] Composition proves \\eqref{eq:two-way-width-new}. For \\eqref{eq:relative-operator-certificate-new}, congruence gives $-\\varepsilon_t\\bI\\preceq \\bV_t^{-1/2}(\\Calg_t-\\bV_t)\\bV_t^{-1/2} \\preceq\\varepsilon_t\\bI$; undoing the congruence gives the claimed factors.',
    '[Rank-sensitive refresh bound]\\label{lem:rank-refresh} Let $\\mathcal R=\\{t:\\Calg_{t+1}\\ne\\Calg_t^+\\}$. Suppose that for every $t\\in\\mathcal R$, the negative part $(\\Xi_t)_-=(-\\Xi_t)_+$ has rank at most $r_t$ and $\\lambda_{\\min}(\\bI+\\Xi_t)\\ge1-\\nu_t$ for $0\\le\\nu_t<1$. Then',
    '[Rectangular factor-path certificate] \\label{lem:factor-spd-path} Let $\\Bmat:[0,1]\\to\\R^{m\\times d}$ be absolutely continuous and set $\\bV(\\tau)=\\lambda\\bI+\\Bmat(\\tau)^\\top\\Bmat(\\tau)$, with $\\lambda>0$. Then',
    "[Relative scalar-link drift and centering] \\label{lem:relative-link-drift} Suppose the prediction model has the fixed-feature scalar-link form $\\mu_{\\btheta}(z)=h(\\phi(z)^\\top\\btheta)$, where $\\phi(z)\\in\\R^d$, $h$ is differentiable, and $h'(q)$ is nonzero throughout the certified scalar region. For $s<t$, write $\\phi_s=\\phi(z_s)$ and define \\[ q_s=\\phi_s^\\top\\btheta_s,\\qquad q_{s,t}=\\phi_s^\\top\\btheta_t,\\qquad c_{s,t}=\\frac{h'(q_{s,t})}{h'(q_s)},\\qquad \\rho_t=\\max_{s<t}|c_{s,t}-1|, \\] with $\\rho_1=0$. Here $\\phi_s,q_s$, and $h'(q_s)$ are fixed before reward $r_s$, while $\\btheta_t$ and every ratio above are $\\cHist_t$-measurable before action $a_t$ is selected. Then:",
    '[Roundwise scalar-width invariance]\\label{prop:scalar-invariance} If two width functions satisfy $\\hat s_t(a)=c_ts_t(a)$ for every candidate action, where $c_t>0$ is action independent, then replacing a scalar bonus coefficient $b_t$ by $b_t/c_t$ gives identical UCB scores and therefore the same selected action under the same tie rule.',
    '[Sharp two-sided feature-drift sandwich]\\label{cor:drift-sandwich} If $\\chi_t<1$, then',
    '[Spectral-tail information closure]\\label{lem:spectral-tail-logdet} Let \\[ A_T:=\\bCbar_{T+1}-\\lambda\\bI =\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{t=1}^T\\bg_t\\bg_t^\\top\\succeq0 \\] have eigenvalues $\\nu_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\nu_d\\ge0$, and suppose the frozen features in this sum satisfy $\\|\\bg_t\\|_2\\le G$ for every $t\\le T$. For an integer $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}$, let $\\Delta_{T,r}:=\\sum_{i>r}\\nu_i$, $\\tau_T:=\\operatorname{tr}(A_T)$, $r_T:=\\min\\{r,T\\}$, and $q_{T,r}:=\\min\\{d-r,\\max\\{T-r,0\\}\\}$. Then',
    '[Sub-Gaussian noise]\\label{asm:noise} The noise $\\eta_t$ is conditionally $\\sigma$-sub-Gaussian given the context and action: $\\mathbb{E}[\\exp(s\\eta_t)\\mid\\cG_t]\\le\\exp(s^2\\sigma^2/2)$ for all~$s\\in\\R$, where $\\cG_t := \\cHist_t\\vee\\sigma(a_t)$ is the pre-reward sigma-algebra of~\\eqref{eq:filtration}, available immediately after the action is selected and before the reward is revealed.',
    '[Thompson distance and selected-path length] The integrand in \\eqref{eq:generic-path-length-new} is the classical Thompson Finsler norm on the positive-definite cone \\citep{thompson1963certain,nussbaum1994finsler}. Its endpoint metric is \\[ d_{\\rm Th}(A,B) :=\\inf\\{r\\ge0:e^{-r}A\\preceq B\\preceq e^rA\\}. \\] Equivalently, \\[ d_{\\rm Th}(A,B) =\\left\\|\\log(A^{-1/2}BA^{-1/2})\\right\\|_{\\rm op}. \\] Therefore \\Cref{lem:log-spd-path} gives',
    '[Tightness of the split spectral-tail expression] \\label{prop:spectral-tail-tightness} Fix integers $r,q\\ge1$, masses $H,\\Delta\\ge0$, ambient dimension $d=r+q$, and $T\\ge d$. Let a PSD $d\\times d$ matrix have $r$ head eigenvalues equal to $H/r$, $q$ tail eigenvalues equal to $\\Delta/q$, and all remaining eigenvalues zero, with the head values ordered no smaller than the tail values. Splitting after $r$ makes \\eqref{eq:spectral-tail-split} an equality: \\[ \\log\\det(\\bI_d+A/\\lambda) =r\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{H}{r\\lambda}\\right) +q\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{\\Delta}{q\\lambda}\\right). \\] If $\\Delta/(q\\lambda)\\le1$, then the tail contribution obeys \\[ q\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{\\Delta}{q\\lambda}\\right) \\ge\\frac{\\Delta}{2\\lambda}. \\] Thus dependence linear in the tail mass cannot in general be removed without additional information about the tail rank or spectrum. This is a tightness statement for the information inequality, not a regret lower bound for the nonlinear class. The example is algebraic; to embed it in the bounded-feature bandit class one additionally chooses factors satisfying $H+\\Delta\\le TG^2/\\sigma^2$.',
    '[Two-sided spectral-distortion inflation]\\label{cor:twosided} Adopt the hypotheses of \\Cref{thm:regret} with $\\Calg_t=\\hat\\bC_t$, retaining the \\emph{original nonlinear center} (so $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t$; in the convex last-layer regime under exact optimization, $\\bar\\psi_t=0$ and $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t$), and a uniform CG tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\le\\bar\\varepsilon$ so that $\\alpha_t\\le\\alpha_I:=\\sqrt{(1+\\bar\\varepsilon)/(1-\\bar\\varepsilon)}$. Suppose pre-reward factors $\\rho_{-,t},\\rho_{+,t}$ are certified before action selection and the \\emph{two-sided} sandwich $\\rho_{-,t}\\bCbar_t\\preceq\\hat\\bC_t\\preceq\\rho_{+,t}\\bCbar_t$ holds for all $t\\le T$ with $0<\\rho_{-,t}\\le\\rho_{+,t}<\\infty$; set $\\rho_-=\\min_t\\rho_{-,t}$, $\\rho_+=\\max_t\\rho_{+,t}$, and (to satisfy the convention $u_t\\ge1$ of \\Cref{asm:transfer}) take the transfer factor $u_t:=\\max\\{1,\\rho_{+,t}\\}\\le\\max\\{1,\\rho_+\\}$. Write $\\omega_{\\max,T}:=\\max_{t\\le T}(\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t)$ and $\\rho_+^{\\star}:=\\max\\{1,\\rho_+\\}$. Then, on the intersection of the confidence event $\\cE_{\\mathrm{conf}}$ ($\\ge1-\\delta$) and the certificate event $\\cE_{\\mathrm{cert}}$ (which now also contains the two-sided sandwich event; $\\ge1-\\delta_{\\mathrm{cert}}$), \\[ R_T \\;\\le\\; 2\\,\\alpha_I\\,\\sqrt{\\rho_+^{\\star}/\\rho_-}\\;\\omega_{\\max,T} \\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,T\\,\\gamma_T} \\;+\\; 2E_T \\] with probability at least $1-\\delta-\\delta_{\\mathrm{cert}}$, where $\\gamma_T$ is the frozen-feature information gain~\\eqref{eq:infogain}. Under the standard normalization $\\rho_+\\ge1$ (achievable by rescaling the sandwich so its upper factor is at least one), $\\rho_+^{\\star}=\\rho_+$ and the inflation is the familiar $\\sqrt{\\rho_+/\\rho_-}$.',
    '[Unrescaled current-parameter subset]\\label{prop:window} Let $\\mathcal{S}_t\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,t{-}1\\}$ and $0\\le w_s\\le1$, and define the current-parameter operator \\[ \\hat\\bC_t \\;=\\; \\lambda\\bI +\\sigma^{-2}\\!\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t} w_s\\, \\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top , \\] using the same relinearized Jacobians $\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)=\\nabla_{\\btheta} \\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_s,a_s)$ that define $\\bC_t$~\\eqref{eq:curvature}. Then $\\hat\\bC_t\\preceq\\bC_t$, so \\Cref{thm:spectral-distortion} applies with $\\kappa_{+,t}=1$ and, in the exact-transfer case, $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ (\\Cref{lem:whitened}).',
    '[Whitened relative feature-drift bound]\\label{lem:whitened} Let $\\bg_s := \\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_s}(\\bx_s,a_s)$, $\\bg_{s,t}:=\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\mu_{\\btheta_t}(\\bx_s,a_s)$, $\\delta_{s,t}:=\\bg_{s,t}-\\bg_s$, and $\\Delta_{s,t}:=\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|$. Using the symmetric (principal) square root $\\bCbar_t^{1/2}$, define the $d\\times(t{-}1)$ whitened column matrices',
    '[Why $W$ is an abstract near-linearity scale] The all-weights MLP used in the nonlinear diagnostics has action-$a$ mean $v_a^\\top\\tanh(Ux+b)+c_a$ and no $W^{-1/2}$ forward normalization. With $\\bar x=(x,1)$, hidden vector $q_i=(U_i,b_i)$, and $z_i=q_i^\\top\\bar x$, the Hessian block on $(q_i,v_{a,i})$ is \\[',
    '[Worst-case uniform spectral guarantee for subsampled GGN]\\label{lem:subsample} Fix round~$t$ and set $m=t{-}1$. Let $Y_s = \\sigma^{-2}\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top$ for $s=1,\\dots,m$, so that $\\bC_t = \\lambda\\bI + V$ with $V=\\sum_{s=1}^m Y_s$. Each $Y_s$ is PSD with $\\|Y_s\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le G^2/\\sigma^2 =: B$. For $\\delta\\in(0,1)$ and $1\\le n\\le m$, draw $\\mathcal{S}_t$ of size~$n$ uniformly without replacement from $\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ and define $\\hat V = \\tfrac{m}{n}\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}Y_s$ and $\\hat\\bC_t = \\lambda\\bI + \\hat V$. Write $L:=\\log(2d/\\delta)$.',
    '\\;=\\; \\lambda\\,\\bI \\;+\\; \\sum_{s=1}^{t-1} J_s(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top\\,\\nabla^2 A\\bigl(z_s(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bigr)\\, J_s(\\bx_s,a_s), \\label{eq:curvature-bar-exp}',
    '\\;=\\; \\lambda\\,\\bI \\;+\\; \\sum_{s=1}^{t-1} J_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top\\,\\nabla^2 A\\bigl(z_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bigr)\\, J_t(\\bx_s,a_s), \\label{eq:curvature-exp}',
    '\\;\\le\\; 2\\,w_t(a_t) + 2\\epslin(t) \\quad\\text{(lower bound in~\\eqref{eq:bonus-app})}.',
    '\\;\\le\\; w_t(a_t) + \\omega_t\\,\\bar s_t(a_t) + 2\\epslin(t) \\quad\\text{(\\eqref{eq:pred-app} at $a_t$)}',
    '\\Cref{lem:path-certificates} proves $\\bar\\chi_t\\ge\\chi_t$, $\\bar\\psi_t\\ge\\psi_t$, $\\bar F_t\\ge F_t$, and $\\bar E_T\\ge E_T$. The one-sided operator relation and \\Cref{cor:gammahat} give $u_t$ and $\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}\\ge\\gamma_{t-1}$, hence $\\bar\\beta_t\\ge\\beta_t$. All quantities are available before selection; when $\\zeta_t$ is prefix measurable, so is $\\bar\\psi_t$. Uniform candidate-action CG accuracy is checked before selection. Substitution in \\Cref{thm:regret} and \\Cref{cor:lam-observable} proves the claim.',
    '\\] Then \\[ \\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\dot\\bV(\\tau)\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2} =',
    '\\] again taking the first term as zero when $r_{t-1}=0$. Replacing $\\gamma_{t-1}$ in the constructive confidence radius by this predictable upper bound is valid. In that version, \\eqref{eq:approx-rank-current-ggn} holds with $\\Gamma^{\\rm tail}_{T,r}$ replaced by any supplied terminal envelope $\\bar\\Gamma^{\\rm tail}_{T,r}$ and with $\\omega_t$ formed from the resulting radius.',
    '\\bVbar_{t+1}',
    '\\bar F_{T+1}',
    '\\bar s_t^2(a)',
    '\\bar\\Gamma^{\\rm split}_{t-1,r}:={}',
    '\\bar\\Gamma^{\\rm tail}_{t-1,r}',
    '\\emph{(a)} $\\Gmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top =\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\bg_s\\bg_s^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1/2} =\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\bigl(\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\bg_s\\bg_s^\\top\\bigr)\\bCbar_t^{-1/2} =\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}(\\bCbar_t-\\lambda\\bI)\\bCbar_t^{-1/2} =\\bI-\\lambda\\bCbar_t^{-1}$. Since $\\lambda\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\succeq0$, $\\Gmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top\\preceq\\bI$, and $\\|\\Gmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2=\\|\\Gmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le1$.',
    '\\emph{(b)} From $\\bg_{s,t}=\\bg_s+\\delta_{s,t}$, $\\bg_{s,t}\\bg_{s,t}^\\top-\\bg_s\\bg_s^\\top =\\bg_s\\delta_{s,t}^\\top+\\delta_{s,t}\\bg_s^\\top+\\delta_{s,t}\\delta_{s,t}^\\top$. Conjugating each summand by $\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}$ and weighting by $\\sigma^{-2}$, the three groups sum exactly to $\\Gmat_t\\Dmat_t^\\top$, $\\Dmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top$, and $\\Dmat_t\\Dmat_t^\\top$. By the triangle inequality and $\\|\\Gmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le1$, $\\|\\Gmat_t\\Dmat_t^\\top+\\Dmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top+\\Dmat_t\\Dmat_t^\\top\\|_{\\mathrm{op}} \\le 2\\|\\Gmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}+\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2 \\le 2\\chi_t+\\chi_t^2$.',
    '\\emph{(c)} By part~(a), $\\Amat_t^\\top\\Amat_t=\\lambda\\bCbar_t^{-1} +\\Gmat_t\\Gmat_t^\\top=\\bI_d$. Expanding the current replay Gram gives \\[ \\Bmat_t^\\top\\Bmat_t =\\lambda\\bCbar_t^{-1}+(\\Gmat_t+\\Dmat_t)(\\Gmat_t+\\Dmat_t)^\\top =\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\bC_t\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}. \\] The two stacks differ only in their bottom block, hence $\\|\\Bmat_t-\\Amat_t\\|_{\\rm op}=\\|\\Dmat_t^\\top\\|_{\\rm op}=\\chi_t$. These identities also hold at $t=1$, with empty bottom blocks and $\\chi_1=0$.',
    '\\emph{(d)} The matrix $W_t:=\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}(\\bC_t-\\bCbar_t)\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}$ is symmetric with $\\|W_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le2\\chi_t+\\chi_t^2$, hence $W_t\\preceq(2\\chi_t+\\chi_t^2)\\bI$. Congruence by $\\bCbar_t^{1/2}$ gives $\\bC_t-\\bCbar_t\\preceq(2\\chi_t+\\chi_t^2)\\bCbar_t$, i.e. $\\bC_t\\preceq(1+\\chi_t)^2\\bCbar_t$; no bound on $\\chi_t$ is used. Both $\\bC_t,\\bCbar_t\\succ0$, so inversion reverses the order: $\\bC_t^{-1}\\succeq(1+\\chi_t)^{-2}\\bCbar_t^{-1}$. The quadratic form at $\\bg_t(a)$ gives $s_t^2(a)\\ge(1+\\chi_t)^{-2}\\bar s_t^2(a)$, i.e. $\\bar s_t^2(a)\\le(1+\\chi_t)^2 s_t^2(a)$. Since $x\\mapsto(1+x)^2$ is increasing on $[0,\\infty)$, replacing $\\chi_t$ by any $\\bar\\chi_t\\ge\\chi_t$ preserves the inequality, giving $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$.',
    '\\emph{(e)} $\\chi_t=\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_F$, and $\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_F^2=\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\bCbar_t^{-1/2}\\delta_{s,t}/\\sigma\\|^2 =\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\delta_{s,t}^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\delta_{s,t} =\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}^2$. Since $\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\preceq\\lambda^{-1}\\bI$, $\\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}^2\\le\\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|^2/\\lambda \\le L_g^2\\Delta_{s,t}^2/\\lambda$; summing and taking square roots gives~\\eqref{eq:chi-primitive}.',
    '\\emph{(i) Deviation bound.} With probability $\\ge 1-\\delta$,',
    '\\emph{(ii) Sufficient sample size.} For any $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, if',
    '\\emph{Adaptive residual stopping (recommended).} In exact arithmetic, for each action monitor the computable Euclidean relative residual $\\|r_I\\|/\\|\\bg_t(a)\\|$ at each CG step (skipping actions with $\\bg_t(a)=\\mathbf0$). Since $\\varepsilon_{t,a} \\le \\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}\\cdot\\|r_I\\|/\\|\\bg_t(a)\\|$ (standard CG bound, with $\\bar\\kappa_t$ a certified upper bound on the condition number of $\\Calg_t$), stopping that solve when $\\|r_I\\|/\\|\\bg_t(a)\\| \\le \\bar\\varepsilon_t/\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}$ guarantees $\\varepsilon_{t,a} \\le \\bar\\varepsilon_t$, hence \\eqref{eq:cg-tol-uniform}. This is conservative by the factor~$\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}$ but requires no access to $\\Calg_t^{-1}$ and adapts automatically to the per-round condition number. A floating-point implementation needs a verified residual enclosure to inherit this certification literally.',
    '\\emph{Centering.} The centering certificate enters \\emph{multiplicatively}, through $\\omega_t=\\bar\\beta_t+\\bar\\psi_t$ inside the sum of squares $S_T$, rather than as a separate additive term. It vanishes ($\\bar\\psi_t=0$) when $\\btheta_t=\\hat\\btheta_t^{\\mathrm{lin}}$ (convex last-layer models with exact optimization), and the corrected-center variant (\\Cref{cor:corrected-center}) removes it in general at the cost of a frozen-feature solve.',
    '\\emph{Exploration term.} Write $S_T:=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\alpha_t^2\\,u_t(a_t)\\,\\omega_t^2$ for the sum of squares of the per-round factors. The exploration term is $2\\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\,\\Lamalg_T\\,S_T}$. A sufficient condition for sublinear regret is \\[ \\Lamalg_T\\,S_T = o(T^2), \\qquad E_T=o(T). \\] Concretely this holds whenever the per-round factors are polylogarithmically bounded ($\\sup_t\\alpha_t^2 u_t(a_t)\\omega_t^2 = O(\\mathrm{polylog}\\,T)$, so $S_T=O(T\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\,T)$) and the realized dynamic complexity is small ($\\Lamalg_T=O(\\mathrm{polylog}\\,T)$, e.g.\\ via $\\Lamalg_T\\le\\Gamdyn_T$ with both the variation charge $\\Valg_T$ and the endpoint $\\log\\det(\\Calg_{T+1}/\\lambda\\bI)$ polylogarithmically bounded). Note $\\omega_t^2$ contains the cumulative squared linearization error through $\\bar F_t$ (via $\\beta_t$) and the centering certificate $\\bar\\psi_t$, so a small $\\Lamalg_T$ alone is insufficient. $\\alpha_t$ is bounded whenever the CG tolerance $\\bar\\varepsilon_t$ is bounded away from~$1$; in the exact-curvature case $u_t=(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2$ is bounded whenever the whitened-drift certificate $\\bar\\chi_t$ is bounded (\\Cref{lem:whitened}); the whitened $\\ell_2$ accumulation $\\chi_t\\le\\|\\Dmat_t\\|_F$ makes this a near-stationarity condition on replayed-Jacobian drift. The unstable-curvature cost is isolated in $\\Lamalg_T$ (through $\\Valg_T$), not hidden in a spectral constant. The factor $\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda}$ arises from the width-sum bound of the dynamic width-complexity lemma (\\Cref{lem:dynamic-potential}, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:width-sum-dyn}); when $G^2\\le\\lambda\\sigma^2$ it is at most $\\sigma\\sqrt2$.',
    '\\emph{Fixed budget (requires a uniform condition-number bound).} If an a~priori bound $\\bar\\kappa_t \\le \\kappa_{\\max}$ holds for \\emph{all rounds}~$t\\le T$, choose $I$ via Eq.~\\eqref{eq:cg-budget} at $\\kappa_{\\max}$ and a fixed target~$\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\equiv\\bar\\varepsilon$. For a \\emph{nonnegative weighted outer-product} operator $\\Calg_t=\\lambda\\bI+\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}w_s\\, \\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^\\top$ the condition number is bounded in closed form, $\\bar\\kappa_t\\le 1+W_tG^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)$ with $W_t=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}w_s$; a fixed buffer of size~$n$ ($W_t=n$, unrescaled) makes this independent of~$T$. For the full-history operator ($W_t=t{-}1$), and for rescaled uniform subsampling with $w_s=(t{-}1)/n$ (so $W_t=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}_t}w_s=t{-}1$), the bound grows with~$t$. For a known finite horizon, a fixed budget at $\\kappa_{\\max}=1+(T-1)G^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)$ is valid but scales as $O(\\sqrt T\\log(1/\\bar\\varepsilon))$; a horizon-independent fixed budget is not uniform, so adaptive stopping or an increasing $I_t$ is preferable (see \\Cref{app:cg}). For a \\emph{general} SPD operator that is not a nonnegative weighted outer product (e.g.\\ a randomized sketch), the closed-form bound $1+W_tG^2/(\\lambda\\sigma^2)$ does not apply, and a separately certified $\\cHist_t$-measurable condition-number upper bound $\\bar\\kappa_t$ must be supplied.',
    '\\emph{Linearization bias.}\\; Define $\\Phi=[\\bg_1/\\sigma,\\dots,\\bg_{t-1}/\\sigma]$ (the $d\\times(t{-}1)$ design matrix) and $\\hat\\rho=(\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_{t-1})^\\top$. Then $\\sigma^{-2}\\sum\\bg_s\\rho_s = \\sigma^{-1}\\Phi\\hat\\rho$. Since $\\bCbar_t \\succeq \\Phi\\Phi^\\top$, we have $\\Phi^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\Phi \\preceq \\bI$, so $\\|\\Phi\\hat\\rho\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} = \\|\\hat\\rho\\|_{\\Phi^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\Phi} \\le \\|\\hat\\rho\\| \\le \\sqrt{F_t}$. Therefore $|\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\sigma^{-1}\\Phi\\hat\\rho| \\le \\bar s_t(a)\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{F_t}$.',
    '\\emph{Linearization remainder.} The bound involves $E_T=\\sum_t\\epslin(t)$, not $T\\epslin$. Near-linear regimes also require the squared-error sum $F_T=\\sum_t\\epslin(t)^2$ (through $\\bar F_t$, hence $\\omega_t$) to be controlled. $\\|\\btheta^*{-}\\btheta_t\\|=O(1/t)$ gives $E_T=O(1)$; $\\|\\btheta^*{-}\\btheta_t\\|=O(t^{-1/4})$ gives $E_T=O(\\sqrt{T})$. The additive role of $E_T$ mirrors misspecified linear bandits, where a uniformly bounded per-round model error commonly contributes an additive regret term that scales linearly in the cumulative approximation error \\citep{ghosh2017misspecified,lattimore2020learning}. The displayed bound requires $E_T/T\\to0$; a nonvanishing uniform per-round error bound alone yields an $O(T)$ additive term.',
    '\\emph{Noise.}\\; \\[ \\left\\| \\sum_{s<t}\\frac{\\bg_s}{\\sigma}\\frac{\\eta_s}{\\sigma} \\right\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\le \\sqrt{\\gamma_{t-1}+2\\log(1/\\delta)} \\] holds for all $t\\ge 1$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$ by the time-uniform vector self-normalized martingale bound \\citep{abbasi2011improved}. Here $x_s=\\bg_s/\\sigma$ is $\\cG_s$-measurable before $\\xi_s=\\eta_s/\\sigma$ is revealed, and $\\xi_s$ is conditionally $1$-sub-Gaussian given $\\cG_s$. Equivalently, the interleaved filtration $\\widetilde{\\cF}_{2s-1}=\\cG_s$ and $\\widetilde{\\cF}_{2s}=\\cF_s$ supplies the required pre-increment measurability. For any candidate action $a$, Cauchy--Schwarz in the $\\bCbar_t$-norm gives \\[ \\left| \\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1} \\sum_{s<t}\\frac{\\bg_s\\eta_s}{\\sigma^2} \\right| \\le \\bar s_t(a)\\sqrt{\\gamma_{t-1}+2\\log(1/\\delta)} . \\]',
    '\\emph{Prior bias.}\\; $|\\lambda\\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bCbar_t^{-1}\\btheta^*| \\le \\lambda\\,\\bar s_t(a)\\,\\|\\btheta^*\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\le \\bar s_t(a)\\sqrt{\\lambda}\\,S$. (Cauchy--Schwarz in the $\\bCbar_t$-norm; $\\lambda\\|\\btheta^*\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}} \\le \\lambda\\|\\btheta^*\\|/\\sqrt{\\lambda_{\\min}(\\bCbar_t)} \\le \\sqrt{\\lambda}\\,S$ since $\\bCbar_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI$.)',
    '\\emph{Remark (Fisher, empirical Fisher, and GGN).} Three curvature objects appear in the literature and must be distinguished. The \\emph{empirical Fisher} is the realized score outer product $\\sum_s\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\log p(r_s\\!\\mid\\!\\bx_s,a_s;\\btheta)\\, \\nabla_{\\btheta}\\log p(r_s\\!\\mid\\!\\bx_s,a_s;\\btheta)^\\top$; it depends on observed rewards and is \\emph{not} the operator used by CC-UCB. The \\emph{true (model) Fisher} is the model expectation of the score outer product, $F(\\btheta)=\\mathbb{E}_{r\\sim p(\\cdot\\mid\\bx,a;\\btheta)} [\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\log p\\;\\nabla_{\\btheta}\\log p^\\top]$. The \\emph{generalized Gauss--Newton} (GGN) drops the residual-times-Hessian term from the full Hessian, retaining only the outer-product Jacobian part that is guaranteed PSD \\citep{martens2020new,schraudolph2002fast}. For the fixed-variance Gaussian working model underlying the squared-loss quasi-likelihood used here, the model Fisher and GGN are identical, and both reduce to the outer-product curvature $\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_s\\bg_s\\bg_s^\\top$ used in~\\eqref{eq:curvature}. More generally, for exponential-family negative log-likelihoods parameterized by natural parameters $z_{\\btheta}(\\bx,a)$, the true Fisher equals the GGN: $J_{\\btheta}^\\top\\nabla^2 A(z)\\,J_{\\btheta}$, where $J_{\\btheta}=\\nabla_{\\btheta}z_{\\btheta}$ and $\\nabla^2 A$ is the Hessian of the log-partition function \\citep{martens2020new,pascanu2014revisiting}. Bernoulli rewards yield scalar weight~$p(1{-}p)$; categorical rewards yield $\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^\\top$. See \\Cref{app:expfam} for the extension.',
    '\\emph{Scope.} The bound is proved for Gaussian/squared-loss rewards. Extending it to exponential-family likelihoods (Bernoulli, categorical) would require a weighted frozen-feature analysis accounting for the data-dependent Fisher weights~$\\nabla^2 A(z)$ and is left as future work (\\Cref{app:expfam}).',
    '\\frac{\\zeta_0}{\\sqrt{\\lambda W}} +\\frac1\\sigma\\Biggl[ 4(1+\\rho_0)B_\\phi R\\sqrt{\\frac{C_R}{C}}',
    '\\frac{\\zeta_0}{\\sqrt{\\lambda W}} +\\frac1\\sigma\\Biggl\\{\\rho_W \\left[\\sqrt{C_RTP_T}+2B_\\phi R\\sqrt{T-1}\\right] \\nonumber',
    '\\ge\\frac{4c_\\mu^2R^4T}{\\sigma^2b_F^2},',
    '\\ge\\frac{4c_\\mu^2R^4}{\\eta_E^2},',
    '\\ge\\frac{4c_g^2R^2(T-1)}{\\lambda\\sigma^2\\bar x_{\\rm drift}^2},',
    '\\ge\\max\\!\\left\\{\\frac{4A_T^2}{\\lambda\\Psi^2}, \\frac{2B_T}{\\sqrt\\lambda\\Psi}\\right\\}',
    '\\hat\\gamma_{t-1}',
    '\\hspace{    } +\\frac{2(1+\\rho_W)c_hR^2\\sqrt{T-1}}{\\sqrt W}\\Biggr\\}, \\label{eq:scaled-tanh-relative-psi}',
    '\\label{eq:Qt-new} Q_t=\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|_2^2,',
    '\\label{eq:approx-onesided} \\hat\\bC_t \\;\\preceq\\; \\kappa_{+,t}\\,\\bC_t, \\qquad \\bC_t \\;\\preceq\\; (1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2\\,\\bCbar_t',
    '\\label{eq:approx-transfer} \\bar s_t^2(a)\\;\\le\\;\\kappa_{+,t}\\,(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2\\,\\hat s_t^2(a),',
    '\\label{eq:bar-update} \\bCbar_{t+1}=\\bCbar_t+\\sigma^{-2}\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)\\bg_t(\\bx_t,a_t)^\\top,',
    '\\label{eq:beta} \\beta_t \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\gamma_{t-1} + 2\\log(1/\\delta)} \\;+\\; \\sqrt{\\lambda}\\,S \\;+\\; \\frac{1}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{F_t}.',
    '\\label{eq:bonus} w_t(a) \\;:=\\; \\omega_t\\, \\sqrt{\\frac{u_t(a)}{1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t}}\\, \\sqrt{\\tilde{s}_t^2(a)},',
    '\\label{eq:bounded-output-residual} R_t^{\\rm col}\\le \\left[8B_\\mu^2+4\\sigma^2\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{2T}{\\delta_R}\\right)\\right](t-1).',
    '\\label{eq:calg-props} \\Calg_t\\ \\text{is symmetric},\\qquad \\Calg_t\\succeq\\lambda\\bI\\succ 0,\\qquad \\Calg_t\\ \\text{is}\\ \\cHist_t\\text{-measurable}.',
    '\\label{eq:cg-budget} I_{\\min}(\\bar\\kappa_t,\\epsCGbar) \\;=\\; \\left\\lceil\\frac{\\log(2/\\epsCGbar)} {\\log\\!\\bigl((\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}+1)/(\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}-1)\\bigr)} \\right\\rceil \\;=\\; O\\!\\bigl(\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}\\,\\log(1/\\epsCGbar)\\bigr),',
    '\\label{eq:cg-err-eta} \\varepsilon_{t,a} \\;:=\\; \\frac{\\|\\bu_{t,a}-\\tilde\\bu_{t,a}\\|_{\\Calg_t}}{\\|\\bu_{t,a}\\|_{\\Calg_t}}, \\qquad \\bu_{t,a}:=\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a),',
    '\\label{eq:cg-quad} (1-\\varepsilon_{t,a})\\,s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a) \\;\\le\\; \\tilde{s}_t^2(a) \\;\\le\\; (1+\\varepsilon_{t,a})\\,s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a).',
    '\\label{eq:cg-rate} \\varepsilon_{t,a} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\|\\bu-\\tilde\\bu\\|_{\\Calg_t}}{\\|\\bu\\|_{\\Calg_t}} \\;\\le\\; 2\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}-1}{\\sqrt{\\bar\\kappa_t}+1}\\right)^{\\!I_{t,a}},',
    '\\label{eq:cg-tol-uniform} \\max_{a\\in\\cA_t}\\varepsilon_{t,a} \\;\\le\\;\\bar\\varepsilon_t\\;<\\;1,',
    '\\label{eq:chi-primitive} \\chi_t \\;\\le\\; \\|\\Dmat_t\\|_F = \\Biggl[\\,\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\delta_{s,t}\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}^2\\,\\Biggr]^{1/2} \\;\\le\\; \\frac{L_g}{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\lambda}} \\Biggl[\\,\\sum_{s<t}\\|\\btheta_t-\\btheta_s\\|^2\\,\\Biggr]^{1/2},',
    '\\label{eq:corrected-near-linear-beta} \\bar\\beta_t^{\\rm corr}:= \\sqrt{\\mathcal G^\\uparrow_{t-1,r}+2\\log(1/\\delta)}+\\sqrt\\lambda R +\\frac{2c_\\mu R^2\\sqrt{t-1}}{\\sigma\\sqrt W}',
    '\\label{eq:curvature-bar} \\bCbar_t \\;:=\\; \\lambda\\,\\bI \\;+\\; \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{s=1}^{t-1} \\bg_s(\\bx_s,a_s)\\,\\bg_s(\\bx_s,a_s)^{\\!\\top},',
    '\\label{eq:curvature} \\bC_t \\;=\\; \\lambda\\,\\bI \\;+\\; \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{s=1}^{t-1} \\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)\\,\\bg_t(\\bx_s,a_s)^{\\!\\top},',
    '\\label{eq:detratio-plus} \\frac{\\det(\\Calg_t^{+})}{\\det(\\Calg_t)} = 1+\\sigma^{-2}\\bh_t^\\top\\Calg_t^{-1}\\bh_t = 1+\\sigma^{-2}s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a_t).',
    '\\label{eq:detratio-xi} \\frac{\\det(\\Calg_{t+1})}{\\det(\\Calg_t^{+})} = \\det\\!\\bigl(B_t^{-1}(\\bI+\\Xi_t)B_t^{-1}\\bigr)\\big/\\det(\\Calg_t^{+}) = \\det(\\bI+\\Xi_t),',
    '\\label{eq:drift-sandwich} (1-\\chi_t)^2\\,\\bCbar_t \\;\\preceq\\; \\bC_t \\;\\preceq\\; (1+\\chi_t)^2\\,\\bCbar_t .',
    '\\label{eq:endpoint-rank} \\Lamalg_T\\le r_{\\rm end}\\log\\!\\left( 1+\\frac{W_{\\rm end}G^2}{r_{\\rm end}\\lambda\\sigma^2}\\right) +\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal R}r_t\\log\\frac1{1-\\nu_t}.',
    '\\label{eq:epslin-t} \\epslin(t) \\;:=\\; \\sup_{a\\in\\cA_t}|\\rho_t(\\bx_t,a,\\btheta^*)| \\;\\le\\; \\frac{L_\\mu}{2}\\|\\btheta^* - \\btheta_t\\|^2.',
    '\\label{eq:erm} \\cL_t(\\btheta) \\;:=\\; \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_{s=1}^{t-1} \\bigl(r_s - \\mu_{\\btheta}(\\bx_s,a_s)\\bigr)^2 \\;+\\; \\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\btheta\\|^2 .',
    '\\label{eq:factor-path-bound-new} \\mathcal D(\\bV)\\le2\\int_0^1 \\|\\dot\\Bmat(\\tau)\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}\\,d\\tau.',
    '\\label{eq:filtration-F} \\cF_t \\;:=\\; \\cF_{t-1}\\vee \\sigma\\!\\bigl(\\bx_t,K_t,a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,A_{\\max}}, \\Omega_t,a_t,r_t,U_{t+1}\\bigr),',
    '\\label{eq:filtration} \\cH_t^-\\;:=\\;\\cF_{t-1}\\vee \\sigma\\!\\bigl(\\bx_t,K_t,a_{t,1},\\ldots,a_{t,A_{\\max}}\\bigr), \\qquad \\cHist_t\\;:=\\;\\cH_t^-\\vee\\sigma(\\Omega_t), \\qquad \\cG_t \\;:=\\; \\cHist_t\\vee\\sigma(a_t).',
    '\\label{eq:finite-d-gamma-new} \\gamma_T\\le\\Gamma_{T,d}:= r_T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{TG^2}{r_T\\lambda\\sigma^2}\\right).',
    '\\label{eq:frozen-gamma-new} \\gamma_T :=\\log\\frac{\\det\\bVbar_{T+1}}{\\det\\bVbar_1} =\\sum_{t=1}^T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\sigma^{-2}\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\right).',
    '\\label{eq:frozen-potential-proof} \\sum_{t=1}^T\\bar s_t^2(a_t) \\le\\left(\\sigma^2+\\frac{G^2}{\\lambda}\\right)\\gamma_T.',
    '\\label{eq:gap-dependent-exact-current} R_T\\le\\frac{16(H_{\\max}^{\\rm gap})^2}{\\Delta_{\\min}} \\left(\\sigma^2+\\frac{G^2}{\\lambda}\\right)\\gamma_T.',
    '\\label{eq:generic-path-length-new} \\mathcal D(\\bV)=\\int_0^1 \\|\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\dot\\bV(\\tau)\\bV(\\tau)^{-1/2}\\|_{\\rm op}\\,d\\tau,',
    '\\label{eq:growing-window-regret-main} R_T\\le2H_T\\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)T L_q(T;\\tau_w)}+2E_T.',
    '\\label{eq:hessian-path-sandwich-new} e^{-\\bar D_t}\\bVbar_t\\preceq\\bV_t\\preceq e^{\\bar D_t}\\bVbar_t, \\qquad \\bar D_t=\\frac{2L_g\\sqrt{Q_t}}{\\sigma\\sqrt\\lambda}.',
    '\\label{eq:infogain} \\gamma_T:=\\log\\frac{\\det(\\bCbar_{T+1})}{\\det(\\lambda\\bI)} =\\sum_{t=1}^T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\sigma^{-2}\\bar s_t^2(a_t)\\right).',
    '\\label{eq:instantaneous-transport-new} \\operatorname{reg}_t\\le \\beta_t\\!\\left[1+\\alpha_t e^{\\bar D_t} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\kappa_{+,t}}{\\kappa_{-,t}}}\\right]\\bar s_t(a_t) +2b_t(a_t)+\\xi_t.',
    '\\label{eq:inverse-quadratic-identity-new} \\bq^\\top\\Calg^{-1}\\bq =\\bq^\\top\\tilde\\bu+\\tilde\\bu^\\top\\br +\\br^\\top\\Calg^{-1}\\br.',
    '\\label{eq:inverse-quadratic-interval-new} L=\\bq^\\top\\tilde\\bu+\\tilde\\bu^\\top\\br \\le\\bq^\\top\\Calg^{-1}\\bq \\le L+\\frac{\\|\\br\\|_2^2}{\\lambda_{\\Calg}}=U.',
    '\\label{eq:lam-alg} \\Lamalg_T \\;:=\\; \\sum_{t=1}^T \\log\\!\\bigl(1+\\sigma^{-2}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a_t)\\bigr).',
    '\\label{eq:lam-identity} \\Lamalg_T \\;=\\; \\log\\frac{\\det(\\Calg_{T+1})}{\\det(\\lambda\\bI)} \\;-\\; \\sum_{t=1}^T \\log\\det(\\bI+\\Xi_t).',
    '\\label{eq:lam-observable} \\Lamalg_T \\;\\le\\; \\widehat{\\Lamalg}_T \\;:=\\; \\sum_{t=1}^T \\log\\!\\Bigl(1+\\frac{\\tilde s_t^2(a_t)}{\\sigma^2\\,(1-\\bar\\varepsilon_t)}\\Bigr).',
    '\\label{eq:local-additive-new} (1-\\chi_t)^2\\bVbar_t\\preceq\\bV_t \\preceq(1+\\chi_t)^2\\bVbar_t,',
    '\\label{eq:log-path-sandwich-new} e^{-\\mathcal D(\\bV)}\\bV(0)\\preceq\\bV(1) \\preceq e^{\\mathcal D(\\bV)}\\bV(0).',
    '\\label{eq:main-dynamic} \\sum_{t=1}^T s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a_t) \\le(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)\\Lamalg_T.',
    '\\label{eq:main-lambda} \\Lamalg_T:=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\sigma^{-2}s_{\\Calg,t}^2(a_t)\\right).',
    '\\label{eq:near-linear-regret-gamma} R_T\\le2\\alpha_I\\sqrt{\\rho_+/\\rho_-}\\,\\omega_{\\max,T}^{\\rm op} \\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)T\\gamma_T}+2\\bar E_T',
    '\\label{eq:near-linear-regret-main} R_T\\le2\\alpha_I\\sqrt{\\rho_+/\\rho_-}\\,\\Omega_{T,r} \\sqrt{(\\sigma^2+G^2/\\lambda)T\\Gamma_{T,r}}+2\\bar E_T,',
    '\\label{eq:new-noise-condition} \\mathbb E[\\exp(u\\eta_t)\\mid\\cHist_t] \\le\\exp(u^2\\sigma^2/2).',
    '\\label{eq:onesided-C} \\bC_t \\;\\preceq\\; (1+\\chi_t)^2\\,\\bCbar_t, \\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad \\bar s_t^2(a)\\;\\le\\;(1+\\chi_t)^2\\,s_t^2(a)\\ \\ \\forall a.',
    '\\label{eq:operational-transfer-main} \\bar\\chi_t=\\frac{L_g\\sqrt{Q_t}}{\\sigma\\sqrt\\lambda},\\qquad u_t=\\kappa_{+,t}(1+\\bar\\chi_t)^2,',
    '\\label{eq:operator-sandwich-new} 0<\\kappa_{-,t}\\le\\kappa_{+,t}<\\infty,\\qquad \\kappa_{-,t}\\bV_t\\preceq\\Calg_t\\preceq\\kappa_{+,t}\\bV_t.',
    '\\label{eq:operator-width-proof} s_t(a)\\le\\sqrt{\\kappa_{+,t}}s_{\\Calg,t}(a),\\qquad s_{\\Calg,t}(a)\\le\\kappa_{-,t}^{-1/2}s_t(a).',
    '\\label{eq:path-stats-main} Q_t=J_{<t}+n_t\\|\\btheta_t-\\bar\\btheta_{<t}\\|^2, \\qquad R_t^{\\rm col}=\\sum_{s<t}c_s^2.',
    '\\label{eq:path-width-proof} \\bar s_t(a)\\le e^{\\bar D_t/2}s_t(a),\\qquad s_t(a)\\le e^{\\bar D_t/2}\\bar s_t(a).',
    '\\label{eq:projected-width} \\bg_t(a)^\\top\\bC_t^{-1}\\bg_t(a) =\\mathbf z_t(a)^\\top\\!\\left(\\lambda I_{r_U} +\\sigma^{-2}\\sum_{s<t}\\mathbf z_{t,s}\\mathbf z_{t,s}^\\top \\right)^{-1}\\!\\mathbf z_t(a),',
    '\\label{eq:psi-def} \\psi_t \\;:=\\; \\frac{\\zeta_t}{\\sqrt{\\lambda}} \\;+\\; \\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}},',
    '\\label{eq:rank-gamma-main} \\Gamma_{T,r}=r_T\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{TG^2} {r_T\\lambda\\sigma^2}\\right),\\qquad r_T=\\min\\{r,T\\}.',
    '\\label{eq:rank-logdet} \\log\\det(\\bI+A/\\lambda)\\le \\min\\!\\left\\{r\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}A}{r\\lambda}\\right), k(q_A)d_{{\\rm eff},\\lambda}(A)\\right\\},',
    '\\label{eq:rank-refresh} \\Valg_T\\le\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal R}r_t\\log\\frac1{1-\\nu_t}.',
    '\\label{eq:regret-bound} R_T \\;\\le\\; 2\\sqrt{(\\sigma^2 + G^2/\\lambda)\\;\\Lamalg_T\\;S_T} \\;+\\; 2\\,E_T,',
    '\\label{eq:regret-definition-new} \\operatorname{reg}_t :=\\sup_{a\\in\\cA_t}\\mu_t^*(a)-\\mu_t^*(a_t), \\qquad R_T=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\operatorname{reg}_t.',
    '\\label{eq:regret-round} \\mu^*(a_t^*)-\\mu^*(a_t) \\;\\le\\; 2\\,\\alpha_t\\,\\omega_t\\,\\sqrt{u_t(a_t)}\\,s_{\\Calg,t}(a_t) \\;+\\; 2\\epslin(t).',
    '\\label{eq:relative-link-D} \\Dmat_t=\\Gmat_t\\operatorname{Diag}(\\mathbf c_t-\\mathbf{1}),\\qquad \\chi_t\\le\\|\\Gmat_t\\|_{\\rm op}\\rho_t\\le\\rho_t.',
    '\\label{eq:relative-link-M} M_t=\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{s<t}v_{s,t}\\bg_s,\\qquad \\|M_t\\|_{\\bCbar_t^{-1}}\\le\\frac{\\|\\bv_t\\|_2}{\\sigma}.',
    "\\label{eq:relative-link-gradients} \\bg_s=h'(q_s)\\phi_s,\\qquad \\bg_{s,t}=h'(q_{s,t})\\phi_s=c_{s,t}\\bg_s,\\qquad \\delta_{s,t}=(c_{s,t}-1)\\bg_s.",
